window.EXAM_QUESTIONS = [{"id": 4, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 34-year-old man with untreated HIV infection and a CD4 count of 40 has progressive dyspnoea and exertional desaturation. The radiograph is near normal. HRCT shows bilateral perihilar ground-glass opacity with sparing of the subpleural lung and several thin-walled cysts. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis with geographic crazy-paving change across the mid zones", "B": "Cytomegalovirus pneumonitis with scattered random nodules and dense lobar consolidation", "C": "Hypersensitivity pneumonitis with centrilobular nodules and mosaic attenuation from air trapping", "D": "Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia with secondary thin-walled cyst formation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia with secondary thin-walled cyst formation): Perihilar ground-glass change with subpleural sparing and thin-walled cysts in a profoundly immunosuppressed patient is classic Pneumocystis infection. Cytomegalovirus is uncommon and tends to nodules or consolidation. Alveolar proteinosis gives crazy-paving but does not fit the clinical context. Hypersensitivity pneumonitis requires an antigen exposure and shows nodules with air trapping."}, {"id": 7, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 52-year-old man with previous treated tuberculosis has recurrent haemoptysis. CT shows a thick-walled upper lobe cavity containing a mobile soft tissue mass, with a crescent of air above it that shifts when he lies prone. The pleura adjacent to the cavity is thickened. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis with central bronchiectasis and high-attenuation mucus plugs", "B": "Cavitating lung abscess with an air-fluid level and surrounding consolidation", "C": "Angioinvasive aspergillosis with a nodule surrounded by a halo of ground-glass opacity", "D": "Aspergilloma (mycetoma) colonising a pre-existing post-tuberculous upper lobe cavity"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Aspergilloma (mycetoma) colonising a pre-existing post-tuberculous upper lobe cavity): A mobile intracavitary mass with a gravity-dependent air crescent in an old cavity is a fungus ball. Angioinvasive disease occurs in neutropenic patients and shows a halo sign. Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis shows central bronchiectasis and mucus impaction. An abscess has an air-fluid level and does not contain a mobile solid ball."}, {"id": 11, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 52-year-old woman with Raynaud phenomenon and positive anti-Scl-70 antibodies has progressive dyspnoea. HRCT shows symmetrical basal ground-glass opacity with fine reticulation and traction bronchiectasis, and a thin band of normal lung immediately beneath the pleura. A dilated oesophagus is noted. Which pattern is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Desquamative interstitial pneumonia with diffuse ground-glass change and small cysts in smokers", "B": "Usual interstitial pneumonia with honeycombing in a subpleural basal distribution", "C": "Fibrotic nonspecific interstitial pneumonia associated with systemic sclerosis", "D": "Lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia with thin-walled cysts and centrilobular nodules in Sjogren syndrome"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Fibrotic nonspecific interstitial pneumonia associated with systemic sclerosis): Subpleural sparing with basal ground-glass, reticulation and traction bronchiectasis in systemic sclerosis points to NSIP, the commonest interstitial pattern in that disease. UIP would show honeycombing reaching the pleura. Desquamative interstitial pneumonia is linked to smoking. Lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia is associated with Sjogren syndrome and shows cysts and nodules."}, {"id": 15, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 38-year-old non-smoker has progressive dyspnoea and airflow obstruction with a family history of early lung disease. CT shows diffuse low attenuation in the lower lobes with paucity of vessels and a few lower lobe cysts. Which test is the most appropriate next step to confirm the likely cause?", "options": {"A": "Serum VEGF-D level to confirm lymphangioleiomyomatosis as the cause", "B": "Serum alpha-1 antitrypsin level with phenotype or genotype testing", "C": "High-resolution CT with expiratory imaging for small airways obstruction", "D": "FLCN gene analysis to confirm Birt-Hogg-Dube syndrome as the cause"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Serum alpha-1 antitrypsin level with phenotype or genotype testing): Early onset basal emphysema with a family history in a non-smoker points to alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, confirmed by serum level and phenotype or genotype. VEGF-D suits lymphangioleiomyomatosis, which gives diffuse thin-walled cysts rather than basal emphysema. FLCN testing suits Birt-Hogg-Dube, with lower zone cysts and pneumothoraces. Further CT would describe the disease but not name its cause."}, {"id": 16, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 50-year-old woman has chronic productive cough with recurrent chest infections. HRCT shows bronchial wall thickening and mucus plugging in the right middle lobe, with some volume loss in that lobe. Bronchiectasis is suspected rather than chronic bronchitis. Which additional finding on the same HRCT most reliably establishes the diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Right middle lobe volume loss with crowding of bronchovascular bundles", "B": "Bronchoarterial ratio greater than one with lack of bronchial tapering", "C": "Tree-in-bud nodularity in the peripheral subsegmental airways of the same lobe", "D": "Mosaic attenuation with air trapping on expiratory images of the lung"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Bronchoarterial ratio greater than one with lack of bronchial tapering): Bronchiectasis is defined by bronchial dilatation, shown as a bronchoarterial ratio above one and failure of the airway to taper. Tree-in-bud and air trapping commonly accompany it but do not define it. Volume loss with crowding occurs in collapse and in bronchiectasis alike, so it does not establish the diagnosis."}, {"id": 17, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 55-year-old woman, a lifelong non-smoker, has an incidental solitary 5 mm solid nodule in the left lower lobe on CT. There is no emphysema or family history of lung cancer. According to Fleischner Society 2017 guidance, what is the recommended management?", "options": {"A": "Repeat CT at 6 to 12 months, then again at 18 to 24 months", "B": "Repeat CT at 12 months, then stop if the nodule is stable", "C": "Perform PET-CT or tissue sampling given the smoking history", "D": "No routine follow-up is needed, as risk is low at this size"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (No routine follow-up is needed, as risk is low at this size): Fleischner 2017 advises no routine follow-up for solid nodules under 6 mm in low-risk patients, and this lifelong non-smoker has no risk features. An optional 12 month CT applies only to high-risk patients. Interval CT at 6 to 12 months applies to 6 to 8 mm nodules. PET has poor sensitivity below 8 mm."}, {"id": 21, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 59-year-old non-smoking woman has a 22 mm ground-glass nodule in the right upper lobe that has been stable for four years but now shows a new 5 mm solid centre, with bubble-like lucencies. Which pathological process best explains this evolution?", "options": {"A": "Focal organising pneumonia with progressive consolidation and early scarring", "B": "Atypical adenomatous hyperplasia with slow enlargement and developing fibrosis", "C": "Progression of adenocarcinoma in situ toward invasive adenocarcinoma with lepidic growth", "D": "Low grade marginal zone lymphoma of mucosa-associated tissue with air bronchograms"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Progression of adenocarcinoma in situ toward invasive adenocarcinoma with lepidic growth): A persistent ground-glass nodule that develops a solid component and bubble-like lucencies reflects progression from in situ to invasive adenocarcinoma. Organising pneumonia changes within weeks to months. Marginal zone lymphoma is usually consolidative with air bronchograms. Atypical adenomatous hyperplasia is typically under 5 mm, so a 22 mm lesion is not explained by it."}, {"id": 26, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 70-year-old retired plumber has gradual breathlessness. CT shows a right circumferential nodular pleural thickening of 1.5 cm involving the mediastinal pleura, with a small effusion and a shrunken ipsilateral hemithorax. Fixed volume loss is evident. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Pleural lymphoma with nodular pleural thickening and mediastinal lymphadenopathy", "B": "Malignant pleural mesothelioma related to prior asbestos exposure", "C": "Benign asbestos-related diffuse pleural thickening with blunting of the costophrenic angle", "D": "Pleural metastases from adenocarcinoma with nodular circumferential pleural thickening"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Malignant pleural mesothelioma related to prior asbestos exposure): Circumferential nodular pleural thickening over 1 cm with mediastinal pleural involvement and a shrunken hemithorax in an asbestos-exposed man is mesothelioma. Metastases can mimic it, but the exposure history favours mesothelioma. Pleural lymphoma is rare and usually shows prominent nodes. Benign diffuse thickening is smooth, tends to spare the mediastinal pleura and is not nodular."}, {"id": 28, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 57-year-old man worked 25 years as a sandblaster and has gradual exertional breathlessness. The chest radiograph shows numerous small nodules in the upper zones and bilateral hilar enlargement, with thin curvilinear calcification at the periphery of some nodes. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Coal workers pneumoconiosis with upper zone nodules and progressive massive fibrosis", "B": "Chronic simple silicosis with eggshell calcification of hilar lymph nodes", "C": "Sarcoidosis with perilymphatic nodules and bilateral hilar lymph node enlargement", "D": "Berylliosis with perilymphatic nodules and hilar adenopathy mimicking sarcoidosis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Chronic simple silicosis with eggshell calcification of hilar lymph nodes): Upper zone small nodules with hilar adenopathy and eggshell nodal calcification in a sandblaster indicate silicosis. Sarcoidosis gives similar nodes and nodules but rarely eggshell calcification and has no silica exposure. Berylliosis needs beryllium exposure. Coal workers pneumoconiosis follows coal dust and tends to cause massive fibrosis."}, {"id": 29, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 62-year-old retired coal miner has progressive dyspnoea. The chest radiograph shows bilateral upper zone conglomerate masses over 1 cm, with background small nodules, and the masses migrate toward the hila over time. Which mechanism and complication are best matched?", "options": {"A": "Rheumatoid pneumoconiosis, with multiple peripheral nodules and risk of pneumothorax from rupture", "B": "Sarcoid conglomerate masses, with airway distortion and risk of fungal ball formation", "C": "Lung cancer, with a spiculated mass and risk of contralateral nodal metastasis", "D": "Progressive massive fibrosis, with a risk of cavitation and superimposed mycobacterial infection"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Progressive massive fibrosis, with a risk of cavitation and superimposed mycobacterial infection): Upper zone conglomerate masses in a coal worker are progressive massive fibrosis, which migrate toward the hila, can cavitate and predispose to tuberculosis. Caplan syndrome shows peripheral nodules in a patient with rheumatoid arthritis. Bilateral symmetrical masses in this setting are not carcinoma. Sarcoid masses are possible but the exposure history points to pneumoconiosis."}, {"id": 31, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 24-year-old woman has vague chest discomfort. CT shows a 7 cm anterior mediastinal mass with a thick wall, containing fat attenuation, a focus of calcification resembling a tooth and a fluid component. Tumour markers are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Thymolipoma, an encapsulated fatty mass draped over the heart without calcification", "B": "Seminoma, a homogeneous lobulated mass with raised beta human chorionic gonadotropin", "C": "Non-seminomatous germ cell tumour, a heterogeneous mass with necrosis and elevated alpha-fetoprotein", "D": "Mature cystic teratoma, a benign germ cell tumour"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Mature cystic teratoma, a benign germ cell tumour): Fat, calcification resembling a tooth and a cystic component in an anterior mediastinal mass are diagnostic of mature teratoma, and normal markers support benignity. Seminoma is homogeneous without fat. Thymolipoma contains fat but not teeth or cystic elements. Non-seminomatous tumours have raised markers and aggressive features."}, {"id": 37, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A frontal chest radiograph in an adult with cough shows loss of the left heart border while the left hemidiaphragm and aortic knob remain sharply visible. A lateral view would be expected to localise the abnormal opacity to which segment of the lung?", "options": {"A": "The lingula of the left upper lobe, adjacent to the left heart border", "B": "The posterior segment of the left upper lobe, adjacent to the aortic arch", "C": "The superior segment of the left lower lobe, adjacent to the descending aorta", "D": "The anterior basal segment of the left lower lobe, adjacent to the hemidiaphragm"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The lingula of the left upper lobe, adjacent to the left heart border): The silhouette sign says that an opacity touching a border of the same density obliterates that border. The left heart border abuts the lingula, so lingular disease removes it while the aortic knob and diaphragm remain visible. Posterior segment disease would erase the aortic arch. Lower lobe disease affects the diaphragm or descending aorta."}, {"id": 38, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 48-year-old woman with recurrent middle lobe infections has an ill-defined opacity at the right heart border on a frontal film. The lateral film shows a thin wedge-shaped band between the horizontal and oblique fissures converging at the hilum. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Loculated fluid within the minor fissure forming a pseudotumour with tapering ends", "B": "Right middle lobe consolidation with air bronchograms and preserved lobar volume", "C": "Right lower lobe collapse, with a triangular retrocardiac opacity and obscured diaphragm outline", "D": "Right middle lobe collapse, which may be caused by a narrow lobar bronchus"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Right middle lobe collapse, which may be caused by a narrow lobar bronchus): Middle lobe collapse obscures the right heart border and appears on the lateral film as a thin wedge between the minor and major fissures converging at the hilum. Consolidation keeps volume and shows air bronchograms. Lower lobe collapse obscures the diaphragm instead. A loculated fissural effusion is lens shaped and leaves the heart border visible."}, {"id": 43, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 32-year-old heavy smoker presents with a spontaneous pneumothorax and mild exertional dyspnoea. HRCT shows bizarre-shaped thick- and thin-walled cysts and small nodules, some cavitating, concentrated in the upper and mid zones with sparing of the costophrenic angles. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis, a smoking-related interstitial lung disease", "B": "Birt-Hogg-Dube syndrome, with lower lobe subpleural cysts and renal tumours", "C": "Lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia, with perivascular cysts and ground-glass opacity in Sjogren syndrome", "D": "Lymphangioleiomyomatosis, with uniform thin-walled cysts distributed diffusely in a young woman"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis, a smoking-related interstitial lung disease): Bizarre cysts and nodules sparing the costophrenic angles in a young smoker are typical of pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis. Lymphangioleiomyomatosis affects women and shows uniform round cysts without sparing. Birt-Hogg-Dube cysts are basal and subpleural. Lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia is linked to Sjogren syndrome."}, {"id": 44, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 35-year-old woman has progressive dyspnoea and recurrent chylous pleural effusions. HRCT shows numerous thin-walled round cysts of uniform size distributed diffusely, without any nodules, and CT of the abdomen shows a fat-containing renal mass. What is the likely unifying diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Langerhans cell histiocytosis associated with diabetes insipidus and lytic bone lesions", "B": "Sjogren-related lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia associated with salivary gland enlargement", "C": "Lymphangioleiomyomatosis associated with renal angiomyolipoma, linked to tuberous sclerosis", "D": "Birt-Hogg-Dube syndrome associated with renal chromophobe tumours and facial fibrofolliculomas"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lymphangioleiomyomatosis associated with renal angiomyolipoma, linked to tuberous sclerosis): Diffuse uniform thin-walled cysts with chylous effusions and a fat-containing renal mass indicate lymphangioleiomyomatosis with angiomyolipoma, as in tuberous sclerosis. Langerhans disease produces bizarre cysts and nodules in smokers. Birt-Hogg-Dube is associated with chromophobe and oncocytic tumours. Lymphocytic pneumonia needs Sjogren features."}, {"id": 49, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 25-year-old man had severe bronchiolitis in infancy and now has mild exertional dyspnoea and a normal clinical examination. The chest radiograph shows a small, hyperlucent left lung with diminished vascular markings and a small left hilum. Expiratory imaging shows air trapping in the left lung. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Swyer-James (MacLeod) syndrome from postinfective obliterative bronchiolitis in childhood", "B": "Congenital lobar overinflation with a hyperlucent and enlarged left upper lobe compressing adjacent lung", "C": "Large left pneumothorax with a visible visceral pleural line and contralateral mediastinal shift", "D": "Left pulmonary artery agenesis with a small hemithorax and ipsilateral mediastinal shift"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Swyer-James (MacLeod) syndrome from postinfective obliterative bronchiolitis in childhood): A unilateral small hyperlucent lung with a small hilum and air trapping after childhood bronchiolitis is Swyer-James syndrome. Pulmonary artery agenesis lacks air trapping. A pneumothorax shows a pleural line and a collapsed lung rather than volume loss with preserved lung markings. Congenital lobar overinflation enlarges the affected lobe."}, {"id": 51, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 63-year-old woman with fever has a large right pleural effusion on chest radiograph and suspected infection. Before any pleural drainage, bedside ultrasound is requested to guide management. Which ultrasound feature would most strongly indicate an exudative or complicated effusion requiring drainage?", "options": {"A": "Large anechoic effusion with compressive atelectasis of the adjacent lower lobe", "B": "Nodular parietal pleural thickening of more than 3 mm with a small effusion", "C": "Anechoic pleural fluid with a smooth thin pleura and no internal structure", "D": "Fibrinous septations or echogenic swirling material within the pleural fluid"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Fibrinous septations or echogenic swirling material within the pleural fluid): Fibrinous septations and echogenic swirling material suggest an exudate with fibrin, typically a complicated parapneumonic effusion needing drainage. Anechoic fluid without structure is more often a transudate or simple effusion. Nodular pleural thickening suggests malignancy. A large anechoic effusion with atelectasis is simple in character and may be tapped for symptoms only."}, {"id": 54, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 63-year-old smoker with haemoptysis has an ill-defined veil-like haze over the left hemithorax on a frontal chest radiograph. There is a lucent crescent between the aortic knob and the opacity, and the left hilum is elevated. Which diagnosis fits best?", "options": {"A": "Large left pleural effusion with a meniscus and contralateral mediastinal shift", "B": "Lingular consolidation with loss of the left heart border and preserved lung volume", "C": "Left upper lobe collapse with the luftsichel sign from hyperinflated superior segment of the lower lobe", "D": "Left lower lobe collapse with a retrocardiac triangular opacity and a depressed hilum"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Left upper lobe collapse with the luftsichel sign from hyperinflated superior segment of the lower lobe): A veil-like haze with elevated hilum and a lucent crescent around the aortic knob, the luftsichel sign, is typical of left upper lobe collapse because the hyperexpanded superior segment of the lower lobe fills the apex. Lower lobe collapse depresses the hilum. Lingular consolidation obscures the heart border. An effusion would push the mediastinum away."}, {"id": 58, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 72-year-old man has exertional dyspnoea, thickened ventricular walls on echocardiography and low QRS voltage on ECG. Cardiac MRI shows biventricular wall thickening, difficulty nulling the myocardium on inversion scout images, and diffuse subendocardial late enhancement. Which feature on parametric mapping is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Markedly raised native T1 with expanded extracellular volume fraction", "B": "Normal native T1 with focal high signal restricted to the apical segments", "C": "Shortened native T2 star values from myocardial iron deposition in the septum", "D": "Regional T2 prolongation confined to a single coronary territory with oedema"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Markedly raised native T1 with expanded extracellular volume fraction): Cardiac amyloid expands the extracellular space, so native T1 and extracellular volume are both markedly elevated, and the abnormal gadolinium kinetics explain the nulling problem. Iron overload shortens T2 star and T1 instead. Regional T2 prolongation fits acute infarction or myocarditis. Normal native T1 would be unusual with diffuse enhancement and this clinical picture."}, {"id": 61, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 41-year-old woman has complete heart block and bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy on chest radiograph. Cardiac MRI shows patchy enhancement in the basal septum and the lateral wall that spares no single coronary territory, involving both mid-wall and subepicardial layers. What is the most appropriate next imaging study to guide therapy?", "options": {"A": "Coronary CT angiography to exclude proximal left anterior descending artery stenosis", "B": "FDG PET after dietary preparation to show active myocardial inflammation", "C": "Cardiac CT with calcium scoring to estimate the lifetime risk of future events", "D": "Repeat contrast MRI with stress perfusion to quantify ischaemic burden in the septum"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (FDG PET after dietary preparation to show active myocardial inflammation): Patchy non-vascular enhancement, heart block and hilar nodes suggest cardiac sarcoidosis, and FDG PET after a high fat, low carbohydrate preparation shows whether inflammation is active and treatable. Stress perfusion MRI addresses ischaemia, which does not explain this distribution. Coronary CT and calcium scoring answer atherosclerosis questions, not inflammatory activity."}, {"id": 65, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 58-year-old man undergoes cardiac MRI four days after primary percutaneous coronary intervention for anterior ST elevation myocardial infarction. Late gadolinium images show a bright anterior wall infarct containing a central dark zone that persists on later inversion times. What does the dark zone represent, and what does it predict?", "options": {"A": "Residual viable myocardium within the infarct, predicting improved contractile recovery on follow-up", "B": "Microvascular obstruction, predicting larger infarct size, adverse remodelling and worse clinical outcome", "C": "Gadolinium washout artefact from partial volume averaging, predicting no significance for management", "D": "Intramyocardial haemorrhage from reperfusion, predicting no change in prognosis beyond infarct size"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Microvascular obstruction, predicting larger infarct size, adverse remodelling and worse clinical outcome): A hypoenhancing core within a hyperenhanced infarct reflects capillary obstruction that prevents contrast entry, and it independently predicts larger final infarct size, remodelling and adverse events. Viable tissue would enhance less, not appear as a persistent core. Haemorrhage often accompanies it but does not erase prognostic weight. It is a real physiological finding rather than an artefact."}, {"id": 67, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 35-year-old woman has exertional breathlessness and a fixed split second heart sound. Chest radiograph shows an enlarged right atrial border, prominent main pulmonary artery, diffusely increased pulmonary vascularity and a small aortic knuckle. Which congenital lesion is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Ventricular septal defect with marked left atrial and left ventricular enlargement", "B": "Patent ductus arteriosus with a dilated aortic knuckle and left atrial enlargement", "C": "Secundum atrial septal defect with left-to-right shunting and right heart volume loading", "D": "Pulmonary stenosis with oligaemic lungs and a post-stenotic dilated main pulmonary artery"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Secundum atrial septal defect with left-to-right shunting and right heart volume loading): Right atrial and pulmonary arterial enlargement with plethora and a small aortic knuckle fits an atrial septal defect, because the shunt loads the right heart and reduces systemic output. Ductus causes a large aortic knuckle. A ventricular defect enlarges the left heart. Pulmonary stenosis would not give plethora."}, {"id": 68, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 76-year-old man has a calcified aortic valve, a small left ventricle and a mean gradient of 28 mmHg with an indexed stroke volume of 28 mL per square metre. Echocardiographic findings are discordant. Non-contrast CT is performed for aortic valve calcium scoring. Which result supports severe stenosis?", "options": {"A": "An Agatston score of 400 arbitrary units, indicating early sclerosis only", "B": "An Agatston score of 900 arbitrary units, above the threshold for women", "C": "An Agatston score of 1,100 arbitrary units, in keeping with moderate disease", "D": "An Agatston score of 2,300 arbitrary units, above the sex-specific threshold for men"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (An Agatston score of 2,300 arbitrary units, above the sex-specific threshold for men): In low-flow, low-gradient disease, CT calcium scoring helps resolve discordance. Severe stenosis is likely above roughly 2,000 arbitrary units in men and 1,200 in women. A score of 900 is below both thresholds, 1,100 is under the male cut-off, and 400 suggests sclerosis or mild disease."}, {"id": 70, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 45-year-old immigrant from a region with endemic rheumatic fever has dyspnoea and haemoptysis. Chest radiograph shows a normal-sized left ventricle, straightening of the left heart border, a double density behind the right heart border, splaying of the carina and upper lobe venous diversion. Which valve lesion is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension with dilated central arteries and oligaemia", "B": "Mitral stenosis with coexisting regurgitation and combined left atrial and ventricular enlargement", "C": "Left atrial myxoma obstructing the mitral valve, causing venous congestion", "D": "Rheumatic mitral stenosis with left atrial dilatation and pulmonary venous hypertension"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Rheumatic mitral stenosis with left atrial dilatation and pulmonary venous hypertension): A normal-sized left ventricle with straightening of the left heart border, a double density and carinal splaying points to rheumatic mitral stenosis. Mixed mitral disease is tempting but would enlarge the left ventricle. A left atrial myxoma can mimic mitral obstruction, yet rheumatic history and the radiographic pattern favour stenosis. Chronic thromboembolic hypertension also causes haemoptysis but does not produce left atrial enlargement or venous diversion."}, {"id": 71, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 59-year-old man treated for tuberculosis in the past has peripheral oedema, ascites and distended neck veins. CT shows a pericardial thickness of 7 mm with calcification, tubular narrow ventricles, dilated inferior vena cava and hepatic veins. Which additional cine MRI finding best supports constriction over restriction?", "options": {"A": "Diffuse subendocardial late enhancement with abnormal myocardial nulling on scout images", "B": "Symmetric biatrial enlargement with normal septal motion throughout the cardiac cycle", "C": "Respiratory septal shift in early diastole, termed ventricular interdependence", "D": "Marked right ventricular dilatation with a reduced systolic ejection fraction"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Respiratory septal shift in early diastole, termed ventricular interdependence): Thickened or calcified pericardium with tubular ventricles suggests constriction, and respiratory septal shift on real-time cine reflects exaggerated ventricular interdependence. Restrictive cardiomyopathy usually shows biatrial enlargement and normal pericardium. Subendocardial enhancement with nulling problems suggests amyloid. Constriction preserves systolic function."}, {"id": 79, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 30-year-old driver is brought in after a high-speed collision. CT angiography shows a contrast-filled outpouching at the aortic isthmus just distal to the left subclavian artery, with an irregular contour and a surrounding mediastinal haematoma. The intima is disrupted. By the Society for Vascular Surgery grading, which grade applies?", "options": {"A": "Grade IV, free rupture, characterised by active extravasation into the pleural space", "B": "Grade III, pseudoaneurysm, usually warranting urgent endovascular repair", "C": "Grade II, intramural haematoma, usually managed with strict beta-blockade and observation", "D": "Grade I, intimal tear, usually managed with medical therapy and short interval imaging"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Grade III, pseudoaneurysm, usually warranting urgent endovascular repair): The grading is I intimal tear, II intramural haematoma, III pseudoaneurysm, IV rupture. A focal contrast outpouching with disrupted intima and contained haematoma is a pseudoaneurysm, which is treated by thoracic endovascular repair in most centres. Grades I and II can be managed non-operatively, and rupture needs free extravasation."}, {"id": 84, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 34-year-old woman with eGFR of 22 mL per minute and a previous severe contrast reaction has pleuritic chest pain and a moderate pre-test probability of embolism. D-dimer is elevated. Chest radiograph is clear. Which is the most appropriate imaging investigation?", "options": {"A": "Catheter pulmonary angiography with digital subtraction in the angiography suite", "B": "Ventilation perfusion scintigraphy, which avoids iodinated contrast and suits a clear radiograph", "C": "MR pulmonary angiography with gadolinium chelate injected at standard dose for suspected embolism", "D": "CT pulmonary angiography with premedication and prophylactic intravenous hydration beforehand"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ventilation perfusion scintigraphy, which avoids iodinated contrast and suits a clear radiograph): With renal impairment, a prior severe contrast reaction and a normal chest radiograph, a ventilation perfusion study is most suitable and likely to be interpretable. CT with premedication still delivers a contrast load. Catheter angiography is invasive and uses iodinated contrast. MR angiography has limited sensitivity for segmental emboli and gadolinium is undesirable at this eGFR."}, {"id": 92, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "Eight days after right pneumonectomy for carcinoma, a patient develops fever and cough producing salty fluid. Serial radiographs showed the right hemithorax progressively filling with fluid. The latest film shows a sudden fall in the air-fluid level with new contralateral opacity and the trachea shifting. What is the most likely cause?", "options": {"A": "Postpneumonectomy empyema with infected effusion and an enlarging collection", "B": "Cardiac herniation through a pericardial defect with mediastinal shift", "C": "Bronchopleural fistula with fluid spilling into the remaining lung", "D": "Pulmonary oedema of the remaining lung from fluid overload"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Bronchopleural fistula with fluid spilling into the remaining lung): A sudden fall in the air-fluid level with salty sputum and new contralateral opacity indicates a bronchopleural fistula, with fluid spilling into the remaining lung. Empyema is tempting with fever but the level would not fall abruptly. Pulmonary oedema explains contralateral opacity but not the level change or salty fluid. Cardiac herniation shifts the mediastinum, usually with collapse, and does not cause expectoration."}, {"id": 97, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 32-year-old woman has cold, painful fingers and a pulsatile supraclavicular mass. A cervical radiograph shows an extra rib arising from C7. CT angiography with arms abducted shows a subclavian artery aneurysm distal to a focal narrowing at the rib. What is the most important complication to exclude?", "options": {"A": "Brachial plexus lower trunk compression producing weakness limited to the intrinsic hand muscles", "B": "Thrombus within the aneurysm with distal embolisation to the hand and digital arteries", "C": "Axillary vein thrombosis with swelling of the entire upper limb after exertion", "D": "Superior vena cava obstruction causing facial plethora and distended chest wall veins"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Thrombus within the aneurysm with distal embolisation to the hand and digital arteries): A cervical rib can cause repeated arterial compression, post-stenotic aneurysm and mural thrombus that embolises to the hand. Venous thrombosis characterises effort thrombosis, and neurogenic compression affects the lower trunk. Superior vena cava obstruction is a mediastinal problem. Assessment of the distal runoff is essential."}, {"id": 98, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 63-year-old smoker has shoulder pain, ulnar arm paraesthesia and a left Horner syndrome. Chest radiograph shows an apical opacity. CT shows an apical mass eroding the first rib. Which imaging study best defines involvement of the brachial plexus and subclavian vessels for surgical planning?", "options": {"A": "Ultrasound of the supraclavicular fossa with guided biopsy, to assess nodal and plexus involvement", "B": "Whole body FDG PET to determine nodal and distant staging in the contralateral chest", "C": "MRI with sagittal and coronal T1 sequences, delineating the apical fat planes and nerve roots", "D": "CT angiography of the thoracic outlet with multiplanar reformats, defining the subclavian artery and rib"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (MRI with sagittal and coronal T1 sequences, delineating the apical fat planes and nerve roots): MRI with multiplanar T1 imaging best shows invasion of the brachial plexus, subclavian vessels and vertebral foramina in a superior sulcus tumour. CT angiography shows vessels and bone but poorly shows the plexus. PET adds systemic staging with limited anatomical resolution. Supraclavicular ultrasound cannot image the apical mass or the roots."}, {"id": 101, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 68-year-old woman has a painless, firm swelling below the right scapular tip. CT shows a poorly marginated soft tissue lesion between the serratus anterior and the chest wall, with attenuation similar to muscle and internal linear strands of fat. A similar smaller lesion is on the left. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Lipoma with homogeneous fat attenuation and a thin capsule requiring surgical excision", "B": "Soft tissue sarcoma requiring biopsy because of an infiltrative margin and bilateral involvement", "C": "Elastofibroma dorsi, a benign pseudotumour needing no biopsy when the appearance is typical", "D": "Metastasis from breast carcinoma requiring systemic staging because of the chest wall location"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Elastofibroma dorsi, a benign pseudotumour needing no biopsy when the appearance is typical): A subscapular, often bilateral lesion in an older woman, isodense to muscle with interspersed fat strands, is characteristic of elastofibroma dorsi, which needs no biopsy when typical. Sarcoma would be enhancing and destructive. A lipoma is uniformly fat. Metastasis would be unusual in this location."}, {"id": 107, "domain": "cardiothoracic", "question": "A 82-year-old woman with severe aortic stenosis is planned for transcatheter valve implantation. Gated CT is performed. The measured annular area is 430 square millimetres, the left main coronary height is 8 mm, and the sinus of Valsalva is shallow. Which complication is most specifically predicted by these measurements?", "options": {"A": "Aortic dissection from balloon dilatation of a heavily calcified ascending aorta", "B": "Pericardial tamponade from perforation of the right ventricular apex by the guidewire", "C": "Mitral valve rupture from direct contact with the delivery catheter in the left ventricle", "D": "Coronary ostial obstruction from displaced native leaflets after valve deployment"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Coronary ostial obstruction from displaced native leaflets after valve deployment): A coronary height below about 10 to 12 mm combined with shallow sinuses puts the left main ostium at risk of being covered by displaced leaflets, so CT planning measures coronary heights and sinus dimensions. Annular size guides prosthesis selection. Perforation, dissection and mitral injury are not predicted by these measurements."}, {"id": 116, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 60-year-old man with cirrhosis has a 5 cm right lobe mass. A right portal vein branch is expanded and contains enhancing soft tissue in the arterial phase, with washout in the portal venous phase and restricted diffusion, in continuity with the mass. Which LI-RADS v2018 category is assigned?", "options": {"A": "LR-M, because vascular expansion with restricted diffusion favours cholangiocarcinoma over hepatocellular carcinoma", "B": "LR-TIV, because enhancing soft tissue within a vein indicates tumour invasion regardless of mass category", "C": "LR-5, because the mass has typical features and the vein is assessed as separate bland thrombus", "D": "LR-4, because venous findings downgrade the category until a biopsy has confirmed malignant tissue"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (LR-TIV, because enhancing soft tissue within a vein indicates tumour invasion regardless of mass category): LR-TIV is assigned when enhancing soft tissue is present in a vein, even if no discrete mass is seen. The expanded vessel, arterial enhancement and diffusion restriction separate tumour thrombus from bland thrombus. LR-5 would ignore the venous invasion. LR-M and LR-4 do not reflect the established LI-RADS rule."}, {"id": 117, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 29-year-old woman has a 4 cm segment VII lesion on gadoxetate MRI. It is homogeneous and mildly T2 hyperintense with a T2 bright central scar, shows intense arterial enhancement and is iso- to hyperintense to liver on the 20 minute hepatobiliary phase. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Fibrolamellar carcinoma, as a central scar with arterial enhancement is typical in young adults", "B": "Focal nodular hyperplasia, as functioning hepatocytes with abnormal biliary drainage retain the agent", "C": "Haemangioma, as marked T2 hyperintensity and a bright central area indicate a vascular lesion", "D": "Hepatocellular adenoma, as typical adenomas retain gadoxetate and appear hyperintense in the hepatobiliary phase"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Focal nodular hyperplasia, as functioning hepatocytes with abnormal biliary drainage retain the agent): FNH contains normal hepatocytes and abnormal bile ductules, so it usually retains gadoxetate and is iso- or hyperintense on the hepatobiliary phase, with a T2 bright scar. Most adenomas lack OATP expression and appear hypointense in that phase. Fibrolamellar carcinoma has a T2 dark scar, and haemangioma does not retain hepatobiliary agent."}, {"id": 119, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 45-year-old woman has an incidental 3 cm segment V liver lesion. On multiphase CT, it shows discontinuous peripheral nodular enhancement in the arterial phase, with progressive centripetal fill-in on later phases and attenuation matching the aorta throughout. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Cavernous haemangioma, with enhancement that follows the blood pool across all phases", "B": "Focal nodular hyperplasia, with homogeneous arterial enhancement and delayed central scar enhancement", "C": "Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, with peripheral rim enhancement and progressive central contrast retention", "D": "Hypervascular metastasis, with early rim enhancement and washout in the delayed phase"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cavernous haemangioma, with enhancement that follows the blood pool across all phases): Discontinuous peripheral nodular enhancement that follows the aortic blood pool and fills in centripetally is typical of cavernous haemangioma. Focal nodular hyperplasia enhances homogeneously and early. Hypervascular metastases wash out rather than fill in. Cholangiocarcinoma shows irregular rim enhancement with delayed fibrous retention and does not track the blood pool."}, {"id": 125, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 55-year-old man has elevated ferritin and transferrin saturation. MRI gradient echo imaging shows markedly low signal throughout the liver. The pancreas is also low in signal, but the spleen and bone marrow appear of normal signal. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Thalassaemia intermedia with absorptive iron overload and expanded marrow", "B": "Transfusional siderosis with iron loading of spleen and bone marrow", "C": "Alcoholic liver disease with mild hepatic iron and normal pancreas", "D": "Primary haemochromatosis, with parenchymal iron deposition sparing the reticuloendothelial system"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Primary haemochromatosis, with parenchymal iron deposition sparing the reticuloendothelial system): In primary haemochromatosis, iron accumulates in hepatocytes and pancreas and spares spleen and marrow. Transfusional siderosis loads the reticuloendothelial system, darkening spleen and marrow. Alcoholic liver disease causes milder iron and does not darken the pancreas. Thalassaemia intermedia shows marrow expansion and usually involves other organs and the reticuloendothelial system."}, {"id": 128, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 70-year-old woman presents with painless jaundice and weight loss over two months. MRCP shows a hilar stricture extending into the confluence and involving both right and left secondary biliary radicles. Using the Bismuth-Corlette classification, which type does this represent?", "options": {"A": "Type I, with tumour confined to the common hepatic duct below the confluence", "B": "Type IV, with tumour involving the confluence and both secondary ducts", "C": "Type IIIa, with tumour extending into the right secondary duct only", "D": "Type II, with tumour reaching the confluence but sparing both secondary ducts"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Type IV, with tumour involving the confluence and both secondary ducts): Bismuth-Corlette type IV involves the confluence plus both secondary radicles, or multifocal tumour, and is generally unresectable by standard resection. Type I stops below the confluence. Type II reaches the confluence only. Type III involves one secondary duct, a for right or b for left."}, {"id": 129, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 32-year-old man with ulcerative colitis has raised alkaline phosphatase. MRCP shows multifocal short strictures and mild dilatation of intra- and extrahepatic ducts, giving a beaded appearance, with pruning of peripheral ducts. Which diagnosis is most likely, and what is the major long-term malignant risk?", "options": {"A": "Primary biliary cholangitis, with a markedly increased risk of hepatocellular carcinoma", "B": "Primary sclerosing cholangitis, with a markedly increased risk of hepatocellular carcinoma", "C": "Primary sclerosing cholangitis, with a markedly increased lifetime risk of cholangiocarcinoma", "D": "IgG4-related cholangitis, with a markedly increased lifetime risk of cholangiocarcinoma"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Primary sclerosing cholangitis, with a markedly increased lifetime risk of cholangiocarcinoma): Beading, pruning and multifocal strictures with inflammatory bowel disease indicate PSC, which carries a high risk of cholangiocarcinoma rather than hepatocellular carcinoma. IgG4 cholangitis can mimic PSC but is not the typical disease with ulcerative colitis and responds to steroids. PBC affects small ducts, so the MRCP is usually normal."}, {"id": 130, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 52-year-old woman has fatigue, pruritus and positive antimitochondrial antibodies. MRCP shows normal bile ducts. CT shows multiple enlarged lymph nodes in the porta hepatis and periportal space, and a mildly nodular liver contour. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Primary sclerosing cholangitis, a large duct disease with multifocal strictures and frequent nodal enlargement", "B": "Primary biliary cholangitis, which targets small intrahepatic ducts and commonly causes reactive portal nodes", "C": "Lymphoma involving the porta hepatis, with homogeneous nodes and secondary liver nodularity", "D": "Autoimmune hepatitis, a hepatocellular disease that commonly causes reactive portal lymphadenopathy and cirrhosis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Primary biliary cholangitis, which targets small intrahepatic ducts and commonly causes reactive portal nodes): PBC affects small bile ducts, so MRCP is normal, and benign periportal lymphadenopathy is common. Positive antimitochondrial antibodies favour PBC over autoimmune hepatitis, which also causes reactive nodes. PSC would show duct strictures on MRCP. Lymphoma is possible but the serology, itch and normal ducts fit PBC better."}, {"id": 134, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 63-year-old man has jaundice and right upper quadrant pain. CT and MRCP show a stone impacted in the gallbladder neck, with smooth extrinsic narrowing of the adjacent common hepatic duct and upstream intrahepatic duct dilatation. The distal common bile duct is normal in calibre. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Mirizzi syndrome, where an impacted cystic duct stone compresses the common hepatic duct", "B": "Pancreatic head carcinoma, where the common duct and pancreatic duct are both dilated", "C": "Choledocholithiasis, where a distal stone causes dilatation of the entire extrahepatic biliary tree", "D": "Klatskin tumour, where an infiltrating stricture involves the confluence with irregular margins"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Mirizzi syndrome, where an impacted cystic duct stone compresses the common hepatic duct): Gallbladder neck stone with extrinsic common hepatic duct compression and a normal distal duct is Mirizzi syndrome. A distal stone dilates the whole tree down to the stone. A Klatskin tumour creates an irregular infiltrating stricture. Pancreatic head cancer produces double duct dilatation."}, {"id": 146, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 48-year-old woman with episodic hypoglycaemia has a 1.5 cm pancreatic body lesion. It is intensely hyperenhancing on arterial phase CT, well circumscribed, and without main duct dilatation or atrophy upstream. Which diagnosis is most likely, and which investigation best problem-solves an equivocal CT for this lesion?", "options": {"A": "Insulinoma, best problem-solved with endoscopic ultrasound when multiphase CT is equivocal", "B": "Insulinoma, best problem-solved with fluorodeoxyglucose PET-CT if CT is equivocal", "C": "Glucagonoma, best problem-solved with endoscopic ultrasound if CT is equivocal", "D": "Insulinoma, best problem-solved with gallium-68 DOTATATE PET-CT if CT is equivocal"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Insulinoma, best problem-solved with endoscopic ultrasound when multiphase CT is equivocal): A small hypervascular lesion with hypoglycaemia suggests insulinoma, and endoscopic ultrasound is the most sensitive localising test for small tumours when multiphase CT is equivocal. Gallium-68 DOTATATE is less reliable because many insulinomas express few somatostatin receptors. Glucagonoma gives a different syndrome. Fluorodeoxyglucose PET is insensitive for well-differentiated neuroendocrine tumours."}, {"id": 147, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 71-year-old man had a nephrectomy for clear cell renal carcinoma eight years ago. Surveillance CT now shows two well-defined hypervascular pancreatic masses, enhancing intensely in the arterial phase, with no duct dilatation or atrophy. Which is the most likely cause?", "options": {"A": "Primary pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma, which is typically hypoenhancing and causes duct obstruction", "B": "Metastases from renal cell carcinoma, which characteristically recur late and are hypervascular", "C": "Autoimmune pancreatitis, which causes diffuse gland enlargement with a delayed enhancing capsule-like rim", "D": "Intrapancreatic accessory spleen, which follows splenic enhancement but is usually solitary and small"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Metastases from renal cell carcinoma, which characteristically recur late and are hypervascular): Late hypervascular pancreatic masses in a patient with prior clear cell renal carcinoma suggest metastases, which often present years after nephrectomy and may be treated surgically. Ductal adenocarcinoma is hypoenhancing. An accessory spleen is a single small round nodule. Autoimmune pancreatitis is diffuse."}, {"id": 148, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 28-year-old man is injured in a motorcycle crash. Portal venous CT shows a splenic laceration, and arterial phase imaging shows a 1 cm contrast blush within the parenchyma, which persists on delayed imaging and has no free peritoneal blood. Under the 2018 AAST splenic injury scale and standard practice, what applies?", "options": {"A": "Grade III injury with a vascular injury, for which angioembolisation is appropriate in a stable patient", "B": "Grade IV injury with a vascular injury, for which splenectomy is recommended in a stable patient", "C": "Grade IV injury with a vascular injury, for which angioembolisation is appropriate in a stable patient", "D": "Grade III injury with a deep laceration, for which observation without angiography is recommended"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Grade IV injury with a vascular injury, for which angioembolisation is appropriate in a stable patient): The 2018 AAST scale upgrades a contained parenchymal pseudoaneurysm or arteriovenous fistula (contrast blush) to grade IV. In a haemodynamically stable patient, angioembolisation is appropriate. Grading by laceration alone misses the vascular injury, and splenectomy is not preferred in a stable patient. Grade III with a vascular injury reflects the superseded scale."}, {"id": 149, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 55-year-old man with atrial fibrillation has sudden left upper quadrant pain. Portal venous phase CT shows a wedge-shaped, peripheral, non-enhancing area in the spleen with its base on the capsule and the apex towards the hilum. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Splenic abscess from septic emboli, with a rounded collection and peripheral rim enhancement", "B": "Splenic vein thrombosis with congestion, splenomegaly and heterogeneous enhancement of the parenchyma", "C": "Splenic infarct caused by embolic occlusion of a segmental splenic artery branch", "D": "Splenic laceration, with a linear low attenuation defect and perisplenic haemoperitoneum"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Splenic infarct caused by embolic occlusion of a segmental splenic artery branch): A peripheral wedge-shaped non-enhancing region in a patient with atrial fibrillation indicates embolic splenic infarction. Abscesses are round and often complicate infarcts. Lacerations follow trauma and are linear. Splenic vein thrombosis causes congestion and splenomegaly, not a sharply defined wedge defect."}, {"id": 150, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 50-year-old man with cirrhosis and portal hypertension has splenomegaly. Gradient echo MRI shows multiple small foci of marked signal loss scattered through the spleen, which exhibit blooming on longer echo times. They do not enhance. What do these findings represent?", "options": {"A": "Splenic sarcoidosis nodules, with low signal on T2 and mild delayed enhancement", "B": "Gamna-Gandy bodies, from small haemorrhages with haemosiderin deposition in portal hypertension", "C": "Splenic metastases from melanoma, with melanin causing variable T1 hyperintensity", "D": "Calcified granulomas from old histoplasmosis, with signal void and dense calcification on CT"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gamna-Gandy bodies, from small haemorrhages with haemosiderin deposition in portal hypertension): Multiple small non-enhancing foci that bloom on gradient echo reflect haemosiderin from microhaemorrhages (siderotic nodules), classically in portal hypertension. Sarcoid nodules enhance mildly and are often hypointense on T2 without blooming. Melanoma metastases are T1 bright. Calcified granulomas can bloom but are few, and portal hypertension is the discriminating setting."}, {"id": 151, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 46-year-old man is five days after orthotopic liver transplantation. Liver enzymes are rising. Doppler ultrasound shows no flow in the main hepatic artery at the anastomosis, and the portal vein and hepatic veins are patent. What is the most important concern?", "options": {"A": "Portal vein stenosis, which causes a high-velocity jet and early graft ascites on spectral Doppler", "B": "Hepatic artery thrombosis, which risks biliary ischaemia and graft loss and requires urgent assessment", "C": "Acute cellular rejection, which is established by a typical vascular pattern on Doppler imaging", "D": "Hepatic venous outflow stenosis, which produces monophasic hepatic vein waveforms and graft congestion"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hepatic artery thrombosis, which risks biliary ischaemia and graft loss and requires urgent assessment): Absent hepatic arterial flow soon after transplant indicates hepatic artery thrombosis, the most common vascular complication and a cause of biliary ischaemia, abscess and graft failure. Portal and hepatic vein stenoses show their own Doppler findings. Rejection is diagnosed by biopsy."}, {"id": 155, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 57-year-old man with compensated hepatitis B cirrhosis and no known liver lesion attends clinic. A registrar is asked what routine surveillance programme best detects early hepatocellular carcinoma in at-risk patients, according to current international guidance. What is the standard recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Multiphase CT every six months, with serum alpha-fetoprotein measurement at each visit", "B": "Ultrasound every twelve months, with serum alpha-fetoprotein measurement at each visit", "C": "Ultrasound every six months, with or without serum alpha-fetoprotein measurement", "D": "Gadoxetate MRI every six months, with serum alpha-fetoprotein measurement at each visit"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ultrasound every six months, with or without serum alpha-fetoprotein measurement): Surveillance in high-risk patients is ultrasound every six months, with or without alpha-fetoprotein, because it is inexpensive and safe. Multiphase CT and MRI are used for diagnosis of observations found on surveillance and are not routine screening tools. Twelve-month intervals miss tumours that grow in the interval between scans."}, {"id": 161, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 52-year-old man develops severe retrosternal pain and subcutaneous emphysema after violent vomiting following a binge. Chest radiography shows a left pleural effusion and mediastinal gas. A fluoroscopic contrast swallow is requested to confirm a suspected distal oesophageal tear. Which contrast approach is most appropriate to start with?", "options": {"A": "Positive contrast omitted in favour of air insufflation at endoscopy", "B": "Dilute barium suspension alone, because it gives the highest mucosal detail", "C": "Water-soluble iodinated contrast first, with dilute barium if no leak is seen", "D": "Oral ionic hypertonic contrast diluted with water to improve safety"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Water-soluble iodinated contrast first, with dilute barium if no leak is seen): A water-soluble agent is used first because leaked barium provokes a severe mediastinal and pleural inflammatory reaction. If no leak is seen, thin barium is then given because water-soluble contrast can miss small tears. Hypertonic agents risk pulmonary oedema if aspirated, and insufflation at endoscopy can enlarge the defect."}, {"id": 162, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 61-year-old woman has biopsy-proven adenocarcinoma at the lower oesophagus. CT of chest and abdomen shows a circumferential mass with no distant disease. The surgical team needs the most accurate assessment of depth of tumour invasion and regional nodal involvement before deciding on neoadjuvant therapy. Which investigation best provides this?", "options": {"A": "Diffusion weighted MRI of the thorax with a respiratory trigger", "B": "Repeat contrast-enhanced CT using thinner sections and oral water distension", "C": "Whole body FDG PET combined with the staging CT scan", "D": "Endoscopic ultrasound with fine needle aspiration of suspicious regional nodes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Endoscopic ultrasound with fine needle aspiration of suspicious regional nodes): Endoscopic ultrasound shows the wall layers, so it separates T1, T2 and T3 disease and allows needle sampling of adjacent nodes. PET is valuable for occult distant metastases but has limited local resolution. Thoracic MRI is not routine because of motion, and CT cannot reliably resolve wall layers."}, {"id": 166, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 45-year-old man with previous laparotomy has acute severe pain. CT shows a U-shaped cluster of fluid-filled small bowel with mesenteric vessels converging to a single point, a swirled mesentery, and reduced mural enhancement in the affected loop. Which finding most strongly indicates strangulation rather than simple closed loop obstruction?", "options": {"A": "Radial arrangement of distended loops converging on a mesenteric pedicle", "B": "Reduced bowel wall enhancement with mesenteric haziness and free fluid", "C": "Dilated loops containing air-fluid levels on a supine radiograph", "D": "Small bowel faeces sign proximal to the obstructing point"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reduced bowel wall enhancement with mesenteric haziness and free fluid): Loss of wall enhancement, mesenteric haziness and free fluid reflect venous congestion and vascular compromise. A radial loop arrangement and the small bowel faeces sign describe the obstruction itself, and air-fluid levels occur in simple obstruction."}, {"id": 173, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 72-year-old inpatient on prolonged antibiotics develops profuse watery diarrhoea and leucocytosis. CT shows pancolonic wall thickening to 15 mm with alternating enhancing mucosa and low attenuation submucosa, trapping contrast between folds, and moderate ascites. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Cytomegalovirus colitis with deep ulceration in an immunosuppressed patient", "B": "Pseudomembranous colitis caused by Clostridioides difficile overgrowth in the colon", "C": "Ischaemic colitis from non-occlusive mesenteric hypoperfusion in an older patient", "D": "Radiation colitis with chronic submucosal fibrosis after pelvic radiotherapy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pseudomembranous colitis caused by Clostridioides difficile overgrowth in the colon): Marked pancolitis with an accordion pattern of trapped contrast and ascites after antibiotics is characteristic of C. difficile infection. Ischaemia is typically segmental, CMV needs an immunocompromised host, and radiation colitis depends on prior pelvic therapy."}, {"id": 175, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 19-year-old with acute leukaemia on chemotherapy has fever, neutropenia and right iliac fossa pain. Ultrasound and CT show circumferential thickening of the caecum and terminal ileum with pericaecal stranding and no appendiceal dilatation. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Right-sided diverticulitis with focal pericaecal inflammation and adjacent fat stranding", "B": "Neutropenic enterocolitis involving the caecum and terminal ileum", "C": "Ileocaecal Crohn disease flare with active mural inflammation", "D": "Acute appendicitis with phlegmon formation in the right iliac fossa"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Neutropenic enterocolitis involving the caecum and terminal ileum): Caecal and ileal wall thickening in a neutropenic patient with a normal appendix is typical of neutropenic enterocolitis. Appendicitis requires an abnormal appendix, Crohn disease has a chronic history, and right-sided diverticulitis would show diverticula with focal inflammation."}, {"id": 178, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 55-year-old man has had five days of progressive right iliac fossa pain with fever. CT shows a 10 mm appendix with a calcified appendicolith and a rim-enhancing 4 cm fluid collection containing gas adjacent to the caecum. Which complication has occurred?", "options": {"A": "Perforation of the appendix with a contained periappendiceal abscess", "B": "Right-sided Crohn disease with an inflammatory ileocaecal abscess", "C": "Perforated caecal diverticulitis with a contained pericaecal abscess", "D": "Caecal carcinoma with secondary appendiceal obstruction and pericaecal abscess"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Perforation of the appendix with a contained periappendiceal abscess): A calcified appendicolith with a gas-containing rim-enhancing collection beside the caecum indicates appendiceal perforation with abscess. Caecal diverticulitis, caecal carcinoma and Crohn disease can all create a pericaecal collection, which makes them tempting. None accounts for the appendicolith and dilated appendix, though a carcinoma should be considered in older patients at follow-up."}, {"id": 179, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 63-year-old woman has left iliac fossa pain. CT shows sigmoid diverticulitis with a 5 cm rim-enhancing fluid collection in the pelvis, separate from the colon, and no free intraperitoneal gas. Using the modified Hinchey classification, which stage and treatment is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Stage III, with immediate laparotomy and washout of the peritoneal cavity", "B": "Stage Ib, with intravenous antibiotics and bowel rest, without drainage", "C": "Stage Ia, with outpatient oral antibiotics and early review", "D": "Stage II, with percutaneous drainage in addition to antibiotics"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stage II, with percutaneous drainage in addition to antibiotics): A distant pelvic abscess is stage II, and collections over about 3 to 4 cm usually warrant image-guided drainage. Stage Ib is a small pericolic abscess, stage III is generalised purulent peritonitis, and stage Ia is a phlegmon without an abscess."}, {"id": 183, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "Staging MRI for a rectal adenocarcinoma shows tumour extending beyond the muscularis propria by 7 mm into the mesorectal fat. The mesorectal fascia is clear and extramural vascular invasion is absent. Using the usual MRI subclassification of T3 disease, which substage fits best?", "options": {"A": "T3b, with extramural depth between 1 and 5 mm", "B": "T3d, with extramural depth more than 15 mm", "C": "T3c, with extramural depth between 5 and 15 mm", "D": "T3a, with extramural depth less than 1 mm"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (T3c, with extramural depth between 5 and 15 mm): MRI subclassifies T3 by depth of invasion: a is under 1 mm, b is 1 to 5 mm, c is 5 to 15 mm, and d is over 15 mm. A depth of 7 mm falls in category c. Greater depth carries a poorer prognosis."}, {"id": 185, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 31-year-old woman with severe ulcerative colitis has fever, tachycardia and abdominal distension despite intravenous steroids, and her white cell count is markedly raised. A supine abdominal radiograph is obtained. Which measurement most supports the diagnosis of toxic megacolon and prompts surgical review?", "options": {"A": "Sigmoid colon diameter exceeding 9 cm with an inverted U shape", "B": "Caecal diameter exceeding 12 cm with a coffee bean loop", "C": "Transverse colon diameter exceeding 6 cm with loss of haustra", "D": "Small bowel diameter exceeding 3 cm with multiple air-fluid levels"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Transverse colon diameter exceeding 6 cm with loss of haustra): Toxic megacolon is defined by colonic dilatation, typically transverse colon over 6 cm, with a toxic clinical state and mucosal changes. The other options describe volvulus, small bowel obstruction and caecal thresholds in mechanical obstruction."}, {"id": 189, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 62-year-old man has vague abdominal pain. CT shows a well-defined mass of increased attenuation fat in the small bowel mesentery, with preserved fat around the mesenteric vessels and nodes, and a thin pseudocapsule. No bowel obstruction is present. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Liposarcoma of the mesentery with fat and solid nodular elements", "B": "Carcinoid mesenteric metastasis with calcification and radiating desmoplastic strands", "C": "Mesenteric panniculitis, a fatty inflammatory process at the mesenteric root", "D": "Mesenteric lymphoma with encasement of vessels and large conglomerate nodal masses"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Mesenteric panniculitis, a fatty inflammatory process at the mesenteric root): The fat ring sign, with preserved fat around vessels and a pseudocapsule, characterises mesenteric panniculitis. Lymphoma commonly encases vessels, carcinoid shows calcified spiculated masses, and liposarcoma has a nodular solid component."}, {"id": 195, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 68-year-old man has an irreducible umbilical hernia with vomiting. CT shows a small bowel loop in the sac with a narrow neck, dilated proximal bowel, and free fluid in the sac. Which additional feature most strongly suggests strangulation requiring urgent surgery?", "options": {"A": "Reduced enhancement of the herniated bowel wall with surrounding fat stranding", "B": "Presence of fluid in the hernia sac on its own", "C": "Proximal small bowel dilatation to 3.5 cm in diameter", "D": "A hernia neck narrower than the herniated bowel loop"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reduced enhancement of the herniated bowel wall with surrounding fat stranding): Reduced wall enhancement with stranding indicates compromised blood supply. Sac fluid, upstream dilatation and a narrow neck occur in simple incarceration and do not distinguish ischaemic bowel."}, {"id": 197, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 28-year-old thin woman has postprandial epigastric pain and weight loss. CT angiography on expiration shows a focal smooth narrowing of the proximal coeliac artery with a hooked, J-shaped configuration and post-stenotic dilatation. The narrowing lessens on inspiration. What is the diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Segmental arterial mediolysis with arterial dissection and visceral aneurysms", "B": "Coeliac artery atherosclerosis with ostial calcification and fixed narrowing", "C": "Fibromuscular dysplasia with beaded narrowing of the mesenteric arteries", "D": "Extrinsic compression by the median arcuate ligament crossing the aortic hiatus"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Extrinsic compression by the median arcuate ligament crossing the aortic hiatus): A hooked narrowing from the superior surface that varies with respiration, in a young thin patient, is characteristic of ligament compression. Atherosclerosis and fibromuscular dysplasia are not variable with respiration and show different patterns."}, {"id": 198, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 19-year-old woman after rapid weight loss has postprandial vomiting. Barium study shows abrupt cut-off of contrast in the third part of the duodenum at the midline, with dilatation proximally and relief on lying prone or left side. What is the likely cause?", "options": {"A": "Annular pancreas encircling the second part of the duodenum and narrowing it", "B": "Reduced aortomesenteric angle after loss of retroperitoneal fat", "C": "Intrinsic duodenal web causing fixed luminal obstruction at the same level", "D": "Malrotation with Ladd bands compressing the duodenum at the same level"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reduced aortomesenteric angle after loss of retroperitoneal fat): Weight loss depletes the fat pad between the superior mesenteric artery and aorta, narrowing the angle and compressing the third duodenum, with relief on positional change. Annular pancreas and webs are fixed lesions, and Ladd bands typically present in infancy."}, {"id": 199, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 25-year-old woman has left flank pain and intermittent haematuria. Contrast CT shows marked distension of the left renal vein proximal to its crossing, with sudden narrowing as it passes between two arteries, and dilated left gonadal vein. Which diagnosis fits best?", "options": {"A": "Renal cell carcinoma with tumour thrombus extending into the renal vein", "B": "Left renal vein entrapment between aorta and superior mesenteric artery", "C": "Renal vein thrombosis from nephrotic syndrome with intraluminal clot", "D": "Retroaortic left renal vein passing behind the aorta with normal calibre"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Left renal vein entrapment between aorta and superior mesenteric artery): Compression of the left renal vein between aorta and superior mesenteric artery produces upstream distension and collateral gonadal veins, with haematuria and flank pain. Thrombosis shows luminal clot, a retroaortic vein is a variant behind the aorta, and tumour thrombus requires a renal mass."}, {"id": 202, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 17-year-old falls onto a bicycle handlebar and develops epigastric pain with raised lipase. CT shows a hypoattenuating line through the pancreatic neck extending across the full thickness of the gland. The patient is stable. Which examination best assesses ductal integrity?", "options": {"A": "Endoscopic ultrasound of the pancreatic head and neck region", "B": "Diagnostic ERCP with pancreatography as the initial investigation", "C": "MR cholangiopancreatography, ideally with secretin stimulation of the pancreas", "D": "Repeat pancreatic protocol CT with delayed phase imaging"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (MR cholangiopancreatography, ideally with secretin stimulation of the pancreas): MR cholangiopancreatography is the noninvasive test of choice for the main pancreatic duct, and secretin stimulation improves detection of leaks. ERCP delineates the duct but is invasive and reserved for therapy. Endoscopic ultrasound is operator dependent, and repeat CT often cannot show a duct disruption."}, {"id": 205, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 75-year-old woman has severe epigastric pain, retching without vomiting, and difficulty passing a nasogastric tube. Chest radiograph shows a retrocardiac gas-filled structure with two air-fluid levels. CT shows the stomach rotated around its short axis within a large hiatal hernia. What is the likely type of hernia?", "options": {"A": "Type II hernia with the gastric fundus alone herniated beside the junction", "B": "Type III combined hernia with mesenteroaxial rotation of the stomach", "C": "Type I sliding hernia with gastro-oesophageal reflux and a patent junction", "D": "Type IV hernia containing omentum and colon but not the stomach"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Type III combined hernia with mesenteroaxial rotation of the stomach): A large combined hernia with stomach rotation and obstruction, presenting as the Borchardt triad of pain, retching without vomiting and failed tube passage, is a type III paraoesophageal hernia. Sliding hernias rarely twist, and type II and IV have different contents."}, {"id": 207, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 58-year-old man has a 3.5 cm right renal cystic lesion on contrast-enhanced CT. One segment of the wall measures 5 mm and several septa enhance and measure 4 mm. There is no enhancing nodule. Using the Bosniak 2019 classification, which category and management approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Class IIF, with follow-up imaging because the walls and septa are smooth and minimally thickened", "B": "Class IV, with resection advised because it contains an enhancing nodule with obtuse margins", "C": "Class II, a benign lesion that needs no further imaging or follow-up", "D": "Class III, because thick or irregular enhancing walls or septa are present"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Class III, because thick or irregular enhancing walls or septa are present): Thick (4 mm or more) or irregular enhancing walls or septa without a measurable nodule define Class III in Bosniak 2019. Class IIF needs smooth minimal thickening (3 mm or less), and Class IV needs an enhancing nodule of 4 mm or more with obtuse margins or any size with acute margins. Class II lesions have hairline thin septa without measurable enhancement."}, {"id": 226, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "Six weeks after renal transplantation, a patient has a new 7 cm anechoic collection adjacent to the lower pole of the graft. It has thin septa, no gas and no wall enhancement, and the graft is mildly hydronephrotic. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Haematoma, which is echogenic in the first week and then gradually resolves", "B": "Urinoma, which usually appears in the first few days after the transplant operation", "C": "Lymphocele, which appears weeks after surgery and can compress the ureter", "D": "Perinephric abscess, which would show gas or a thick enhancing wall plus fever"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lymphocele, which appears weeks after surgery and can compress the ureter): Lymphoceles arise from disrupted lymphatics four to eight weeks after transplantation and are typically septated and simple on ultrasound. They may cause hydronephrosis by compressing the ureter. Urinomas appear earlier and haematomas are echogenic initially."}, {"id": 227, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 66-year-old smoker with painless haematuria has a CT urogram. In the excretory phase there is a 12 mm irregular filling defect in the right mid-ureter with proximal hydroureteronephrosis. Unenhanced attenuation is 35 HU and the lesion enhances. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Urothelial carcinoma, with an enhancing soft tissue filling defect and obstruction", "B": "Fibroepithelial polyp, which is common in older adults and shows an irregular surface", "C": "Radiolucent uric acid calculus, which would be dense on unenhanced CT without enhancement", "D": "Blood clot, which appears hyperdense without enhancement and changes between studies"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Urothelial carcinoma, with an enhancing soft tissue filling defect and obstruction): An enhancing soft tissue filling defect of 35 HU is tumour until proven otherwise. Calculi are denser and do not enhance, and clot does not enhance. Fibroepithelial polyps are smooth and usually found in younger patients."}, {"id": 231, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 63-year-old man has a 2.8 cm left adrenal nodule with unenhanced attenuation of 28 HU. On adrenal protocol CT, attenuation is 95 HU at 60 seconds and 46 HU at 15 minutes. He has no known primary cancer. Which interpretation is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Washout cannot be calculated without a separate venous phase value being acquired", "B": "Relative washout below the 40 percent threshold, favouring a pheochromocytoma", "C": "Absolute washout of about 51 percent, favouring a malignant lesion needing biopsy", "D": "Absolute washout of about 73 percent, favouring a lipid-poor adenoma"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Absolute washout of about 73 percent, favouring a lipid-poor adenoma): Absolute washout is (95 minus 46) divided by (95 minus 28), about 73 percent, and above the 60 percent threshold. Relative washout is (95 minus 46) divided by 95, about 52 percent, above 40 percent. Both indicate adenoma."}, {"id": 233, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 38-year-old man has episodic severe headaches, sweating, palpitations and hypertension. MRI shows a 4 cm right adrenal mass with very high T2 signal, marked heterogeneous enhancement and a central area of cystic change. Which additional finding would best confirm the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Raised 24-hour urinary free cortisol with suppressed ACTH concentration", "B": "Failure of cortisol suppression after overnight low-dose dexamethasone administration", "C": "Elevated aldosterone to renin ratio with suppressed plasma renin activity", "D": "Raised plasma metanephrines in the setting of a T2 bright, avidly enhancing adrenal mass"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Raised plasma metanephrines in the setting of a T2 bright, avidly enhancing adrenal mass): Paroxysmal headache, sweating and palpitations with a T2 bright, avidly enhancing adrenal mass are confirmed by raised plasma metanephrines. Aldosterone excess and cortisol excess are tempting because adrenal tumours cause hypertension in both, but neither produces paroxysmal catecholamine symptoms or this imaging pattern."}, {"id": 238, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "CT of a 63-year-old man shows a confluent retroperitoneal soft tissue mass at the L3 level that lifts the aorta and IVC forward away from the vertebral bodies. The ureters are displaced laterally. The mass is homogeneous with mild enhancement. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Idiopathic retroperitoneal fibrosis, which usually draws the ureters medially and tethers the aorta", "B": "Retroperitoneal haematoma, which would be hyperdense with a rapid change in size", "C": "Retroperitoneal lymphoma, which commonly elevates and surrounds the aorta without constricting it", "D": "Aortic aneurysm leak, with a periaortic collection and wall discontinuity"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Retroperitoneal lymphoma, which commonly elevates and surrounds the aorta without constricting it): Anterior displacement of the aorta from the spine and lateral ureteric deviation are features of lymphadenopathy, such as lymphoma, rather than fibrosis. Fibrosis moulds around the vessels and pulls the ureters medially. Haematoma is hyperdense and acute."}, {"id": 241, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 66-year-old man with a PSA of 9.2 ng/mL has multiparametric prostate MRI. It shows a 1.4 cm lesion in the transition zone with heterogeneous T2 signal and obscured margins, giving T2 score 3. DWI shows markedly restricted diffusion, a DWI score of 5. What PI-RADS v2.1 score applies?", "options": {"A": "PI-RADS 5, since the DWI score is the dominant sequence in both zones", "B": "PI-RADS 4, as a T2 score of 3 is upgraded by a DWI score of 5", "C": "PI-RADS 3, since T2 is dominant and DWI cannot modify the final category", "D": "PI-RADS 2, since a circumscribed lesion in this zone is a stromal nodule"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (PI-RADS 4, as a T2 score of 3 is upgraded by a DWI score of 5): In the transition zone T2 is the dominant sequence, but a T2 score of 3 is upgraded to 4 when the DWI score is 5. Hence the final PI-RADS is 4. In the peripheral zone DWI dominates instead."}, {"id": 245, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 15-year-old boy presents with sudden severe left scrotal pain and nausea. Ultrasound shows an enlarged, heterogeneous left testis with absent colour Doppler flow. The right testis has normal flow. The spermatic cord shows a whirlpool pattern. What is the appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Immediate surgical exploration, without waiting for further imaging or tests", "B": "Antibiotics for presumed epididymo-orchitis with repeat Doppler ultrasound next day", "C": "Manual detorsion and discharge with outpatient urology review", "D": "Nuclear scintigraphy of the scrotum to confirm absent perfusion before surgery"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Immediate surgical exploration, without waiting for further imaging or tests): Absent flow with a twisted cord is torsion, and salvage falls rapidly after six hours, so immediate exploration is required. Manual detorsion may be attempted in some units but is never an end point and needs surgery. Antibiotics suit epididymo-orchitis, which shows increased flow, and scintigraphy only delays operation."}, {"id": 248, "domain": "abdominal_and_pelvic", "question": "A 61-year-old man with a left renal mass has CT showing a filling defect extending from the left renal vein into the IVC. The thrombus enhances in the portal venous phase and expands the vein. Which interpretation is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Tumour thrombus from renal cell carcinoma, enhancing and expanding the vessel", "B": "Bland thrombus from stasis within a renal vein draining a mass", "C": "Primary IVC leiomyosarcoma arising from the venous wall", "D": "Streaming of unopacified blood from the lower limbs mimicking thrombus"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tumour thrombus from renal cell carcinoma, enhancing and expanding the vessel): Enhancement and expansion of the vein indicate tumour thrombus extending from renal cell carcinoma. Bland thrombus is the usual cause of an IVC filling defect, but it does not enhance or expand the vein. Mixing artefact resolves on delayed phases, and primary leiomyosarcoma is rare."}, {"id": 253, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 28-year-old rider is dragged by a horse with her foot caught in the stirrup. She has midfoot swelling, plantar bruising and cannot bear weight. Weight-bearing anteroposterior radiographs show no obvious dislocation. Which finding would be most specific for a Lisfranc ligament injury at this site?", "options": {"A": "Fracture of the navicular tuberosity with a displaced fragment on the medial border", "B": "Avulsion fleck from the dorsal talar head at the talonavicular joint", "C": "Compression fracture of the cuboid with loss of lateral column length", "D": "Small avulsed bone fragment in the gap between the first cuneiform and second metatarsal base"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Small avulsed bone fragment in the gap between the first cuneiform and second metatarsal base): A fleck between the medial cuneiform and second metatarsal base represents avulsion of the Lisfranc ligament and is specific for tarsometatarsal instability. A navicular avulsion or a cuboid compression fracture occur with similar mechanisms but do not involve the tarsometatarsal joint. A dorsal talar avulsion indicates a Chopart injury."}, {"id": 254, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 34-year-old netballer inverts her ankle on landing and reports lateral pain. Radiographs show an oblique fibular fracture beginning at the level of the tibial plafond and extending proximally, with a normal medial clear space and no widening of the tibiofibular clear space. Which Weber type does this represent?", "options": {"A": "Type D, a spiral fracture of the distal tibia extending into the plafond", "B": "Type B, a fracture at the level of the syndesmosis that may be stable", "C": "Type C, a fibular fracture above the syndesmosis with tibiofibular ligament disruption", "D": "Type A, an avulsion fracture of the fibula distal to the syndesmosis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Type B, a fracture at the level of the syndesmosis that may be stable): Weber B fractures begin at the level of the tibial plafond or syndesmosis, which matches the described fibular fracture. Weber A lies below the syndesmosis, usually transverse, while Weber C lies above it with syndesmotic injury. Normal medial and tibiofibular clear spaces favour a stable pattern. The Weber scheme has no type D, and a plafond fracture is a separate pilon injury."}, {"id": 256, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 12-year-old boy twists his ankle playing football and has tenderness over the distal tibial physis. Radiographs show a vertical fracture line through the epiphysis that crosses the growth plate to exit at the joint surface, with no metaphyseal fragment. Which Salter-Harris type is most consistent with this injury?", "options": {"A": "Type III, a fracture through the physis exiting through the epiphysis into the joint", "B": "Type V, a crush injury to the physis with no visible fracture line", "C": "Type IV, a fracture crossing metaphysis, physis and epiphysis in a single plane", "D": "Type II, a fracture through the physis exiting through the metaphysis with a triangular fragment"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Type III, a fracture through the physis exiting through the epiphysis into the joint): A fracture that passes through the physis then turns through the epiphysis to the joint surface is Salter-Harris III, and the absent metaphyseal fragment excludes types II and IV. Type II is the commonest and carries a metaphyseal fragment. Type IV involves the metaphysis as well. Type V is a crush injury and is usually recognised only retrospectively because the radiograph appears normal at presentation."}, {"id": 258, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 31-year-old carpenter has persistent radial-sided wrist pain after falling off a ladder three weeks ago. A clenched fist posteroanterior radiograph shows a widened gap between the scaphoid and lunate, and the scaphoid appears foreshortened with a ring-like cortical outline. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Scaphoid waist fracture with early avascular necrosis of the proximal pole", "B": "Scapholunate ligament disruption with rotary subluxation of the scaphoid", "C": "Kienbock disease with lunate sclerosis and collapse of carpal height", "D": "Lunotriquetral ligament tear with volar intercalated segment instability pattern"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Scapholunate ligament disruption with rotary subluxation of the scaphoid): A scapholunate interval greater than 3 mm with the cortical ring sign of the flexed scaphoid indicates scapholunate ligament rupture and rotary subluxation. Lunotriquetral injury widens the ulnar interval and produces a different instability pattern. A scaphoid waist fracture would show a lucent line. Kienbock disease is a lunate problem and leaves the scapholunate gap normal."}, {"id": 260, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 29-year-old woman has several months of knee pain. A radiograph shows an eccentric, expansile lytic lesion in the distal femur reaching the subchondral bone, with a narrow zone of transition, no sclerotic margin and no internal matrix. The physes are closed. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Non-ossifying fibroma with a thin sclerotic scalloped rim in the metaphysis", "B": "Chondroblastoma, which arises within an open epiphysis in an adolescent patient", "C": "Giant cell tumour of bone in a skeletally mature adult", "D": "Conventional osteosarcoma producing osteoid with a sunburst periosteal reaction pattern"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Giant cell tumour of bone in a skeletally mature adult): Giant cell tumour typically occurs in skeletally mature adults, is eccentric and lytic, abuts the subchondral surface and lacks matrix or a sclerotic margin. Chondroblastoma occurs before physeal closure and often contains calcification. Osteosarcoma produces osteoid matrix and aggressive periosteal reaction. A non-ossifying fibroma occurs in children and is metaphyseal with a sclerotic border."}, {"id": 262, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 15-year-old girl has progressive pain above the knee. Radiographs show a destructive lesion in the distal femoral metaphysis with cloud-like ossification in the medullary cavity, a Codman triangle and spiculated periosteal reaction extending into a soft tissue mass. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Conventional osteosarcoma, a matrix-producing primary bone malignancy of adolescence", "B": "Chondrosarcoma with ring and arc calcification in the cartilage matrix", "C": "Ewing sarcoma of the femoral metaphysis with lamellated periosteal reaction", "D": "Aneurysmal bone cyst with expansile remodelling and fluid-fluid levels"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Conventional osteosarcoma, a matrix-producing primary bone malignancy of adolescence): Conventional osteosarcoma is the commonest primary bone malignancy of adolescence and produces fluffy, cloud-like osteoid in a metaphyseal location, usually around the knee. Ewing sarcoma lacks neoplastic bone matrix and favours diaphyses. Chondrosarcoma shows rings and arcs, and is rare in adolescents. An aneurysmal bone cyst does not produce matrix."}, {"id": 264, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 52-year-old man with shoulder pain has a 6 cm cartilaginous lesion in the proximal humeral metaphysis. Radiographs show ring and arc calcification. MRI shows lobulated high T2 signal. Which feature would most strongly favour low-grade chondrosarcoma over a benign enchondroma?", "options": {"A": "Lobulated T2 hyperintense appearance with thin enhancing septa on contrast", "B": "Lesion location in the proximal humerus rather than the small tubular bones", "C": "Popcorn calcification limited to the central part of the medullary lesion", "D": "Endosteal scalloping involving more than two thirds of the cortical thickness"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Endosteal scalloping involving more than two thirds of the cortical thickness): Deep endosteal scalloping beyond two thirds of the cortex, along with pain, cortical destruction and soft tissue extension, favours chondrosarcoma. Ring and arc matrix and lobulated high T2 signal occur in both entities. Proximal long bone sites are common for both conditions, so location alone does not discriminate. Pain at the lesion site in the absence of a fracture is another useful clinical clue."}, {"id": 266, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 10-year-old boy presents with a pathological fracture after a minor fall onto his arm. A radiograph shows a well-defined central lucent lesion in the proximal humeral metaphysis. A small bony fragment lies dependently at the base of the cavity. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Fibrous dysplasia with a ground glass matrix and rind of sclerosis", "B": "Eosinophilic granuloma with a bevelled edge and a button sequestrum", "C": "Unicameral bone cyst with a fallen fragment sign", "D": "Aneurysmal bone cyst with eccentric expansion and a thin cortical shell"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Unicameral bone cyst with a fallen fragment sign): A central metaphyseal lucent lesion in a child with a pathological fracture is typical of a simple bone cyst, and the fallen fragment sign (a cortical fragment moving to the dependent portion) is virtually pathognomonic. Aneurysmal bone cysts are eccentric with fluid levels. Fibrous dysplasia has matrix. Eosinophilic granuloma may contain a button sequestrum but this sits centrally and does not fall."}, {"id": 268, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 15-year-old boy has shoulder pain. A radiograph shows a well-defined lucent lesion within the proximal humeral epiphysis with a thin sclerotic rim and fine internal calcification. MRI shows low T2 signal within parts of the lesion and extensive surrounding bone marrow and soft tissue oedema. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Clear cell chondrosarcoma arising in an older adult epiphysis", "B": "Osteomyelitis with Brodie abscess and a surrounding rim of reactive sclerosis", "C": "Giant cell tumour, which typically occurs after physeal closure in adults", "D": "Chondroblastoma, a chondroid epiphyseal tumour of skeletally immature patients"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Chondroblastoma, a chondroid epiphyseal tumour of skeletally immature patients): Epiphyseal lucent lesions with internal chondroid calcification in adolescents are chondroblastomas. The characteristic low T2 signal reflects chondroid matrix, and extensive perilesional oedema is typical and can mislead toward infection. Giant cell tumour appears in skeletally mature patients. Clear cell chondrosarcoma is rare and occurs in adults. A Brodie abscess lacks internal calcification and would show a penumbra sign."}, {"id": 269, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 7-year-old boy has fever, a limp and focal tibial tenderness for three days, with raised inflammatory markers. Radiographs are normal and MRI is arranged to look for complications. Which finding would best confirm acute osteomyelitis and help guide the need for surgical drainage?", "options": {"A": "Periosteal thickening with a lamellated pattern and a solid enhancing soft tissue mass", "B": "Confluent low T1 marrow signal with high STIR signal and a rim-enhancing subperiosteal collection", "C": "Low T1 signal limited to the physis with no enhancement after contrast", "D": "Reticular marrow signal alteration with preserved fatty marrow on T1 weighted images"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Confluent low T1 marrow signal with high STIR signal and a rim-enhancing subperiosteal collection): Confluent T1 hypointense marrow replacement, STIR hyperintensity and a rim-enhancing subperiosteal collection indicate acute osteomyelitis with an abscess needing drainage. Preserved fatty marrow on T1 suggests oedema rather than infection. Isolated physeal signal is not diagnostic. A lamellated reaction with a solid mass suggests Ewing sarcoma, the key mimic in this age group."}, {"id": 273, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 55-year-old woman with fatigue, constipation and renal stones has hypercalcaemia. Hand radiographs show lacy resorption along the radial margins of the middle phalanges and tapering of the distal tufts. A lucent expansile lesion in the pelvis is also noted. Which description best fits the pelvic lesion?", "options": {"A": "Lytic renal cell carcinoma metastasis with an expansile component", "B": "Aneurysmal bone cyst with thin cortex and fluid levels", "C": "Giant cell tumour with an eccentric subarticular lytic lesion", "D": "Brown tumour, an osteoclastic lytic lesion of advanced hyperparathyroidism"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Brown tumour, an osteoclastic lytic lesion of advanced hyperparathyroidism): Subperiosteal resorption in the phalanges establishes hyperparathyroidism, and a lytic expansile pelvic lesion in that setting is a brown tumour. Giant cell tumour, aneurysmal bone cyst and renal metastasis all look similar on a radiograph, which makes them tempting. The metabolic context points to a brown tumour."}, {"id": 275, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 66-year-old postmenopausal woman with no previous fractures has a DEXA scan before starting therapy. The lumbar spine T-score is minus 2.7 and the total hip T-score is minus 2.0. According to World Health Organization criteria, which category applies to her bone density?", "options": {"A": "Osteoporosis, because the lowest site T-score is minus 2.5 or below", "B": "Osteopenia, because the total hip T-score lies between minus 1.0 and minus 2.5", "C": "Osteopenia, because lumbar spine T-scores are unreliable in women older than 65 years", "D": "Low bone mass, because the femoral neck and hip are the preferred reference sites"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Osteoporosis, because the lowest site T-score is minus 2.5 or below): The diagnosis rests on the lowest T-score among the lumbar spine, total hip and femoral neck, and a value of minus 2.5 or below defines osteoporosis in a postmenopausal woman. The hip result alone would suggest osteopenia, which tempts readers who use one site. Spine readings can be falsely raised by degenerative change, but none is described here, so the low spine value stands. The scan cannot be called normal."}, {"id": 285, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 42-year-old man on long-term prednisolone has groin pain. Hip radiographs are normal. MRI shows a serpiginous band of low T1 signal in the anterosuperior femoral head, and on T2 weighted images the band has an inner high signal line parallel to an outer low signal line. What is the diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Subchondral insufficiency fracture with a single linear low signal subcortical line", "B": "Osteonecrosis, likely steroid related, with reactive interface around infarcted bone", "C": "Septic arthritis of the hip with joint effusion and synovial enhancement", "D": "Transient osteoporosis of the hip with diffuse homogeneous marrow oedema"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Osteonecrosis, likely steroid related, with reactive interface around infarcted bone): A serpiginous low T1 margin with a double line sign on T2 is highly specific for avascular necrosis, here likely steroid related. Transient osteoporosis causes diffuse marrow oedema without a defined margin. A subchondral insufficiency fracture is a single line. Septic arthritis would demonstrate effusion and synovitis rather than a geographic femoral head lesion."}, {"id": 290, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 30-year-old man is hypotensive after a motor vehicle crash. A pelvic radiograph shows symphysis widening and diastasis of both sacroiliac joints. After pelvic binder application and resuscitation he responds transiently and is stable enough for CT, which demonstrates a large pelvic haematoma with an arterial contrast blush from an internal iliac branch. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Catheter angiography with embolisation of the bleeding arterial branch", "B": "Preperitoneal pelvic packing in theatre without angiographic assessment of the bleeding vessel", "C": "Exploratory laparotomy with opening of the retroperitoneum to control the haematoma", "D": "Surgical external fixation of the pelvic ring as definitive haemorrhage control"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Catheter angiography with embolisation of the bleeding arterial branch): A contrast blush from an internal iliac branch in a patient who remains haemodynamically unwell after a binder indicates active arterial bleeding that angiographic embolisation can treat directly. Preperitoneal packing is a reasonable option where angiography is unavailable but does not target the vessel. External fixation stabilises the ring but does not stop arterial bleeding. Laparotomy risks losing tamponade of the retroperitoneal haematoma."}, {"id": 292, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 37-year-old man twists his ankle playing football and has medial ankle pain. Ankle radiographs show a widened medial clear space with a mildly widened tibiofibular clear space, but the fibula appears intact. He also has tenderness near the knee. Which injury should be suspected, and which further imaging is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Isolated deltoid ligament rupture, with weight-bearing stress views of the ankle", "B": "Syndesmotic sprain without fracture, with MRI of the ankle ligaments", "C": "Weber C fibular fracture, with CT of the distal tibiofibular joint", "D": "Maisonneuve injury, with radiographs of the full length of the fibula"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Maisonneuve injury, with radiographs of the full length of the fibula): Medial clear space widening with tibiofibular widening and tenderness near the knee indicates a Maisonneuve injury, in which an external rotation force travels up the interosseous membrane to a proximal fibular fracture. Radiographs of the full fibula confirm it. A syndesmotic sprain alone would not explain the knee tenderness. A Weber C fracture shows a fibular fracture at the ankle, and an isolated deltoid rupture does not explain the tibiofibular widening."}, {"id": 295, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 66-year-old man with anaemia and back pain has multiple well-defined lytic lesions of the skull and spine on skeletal survey. Technetium bone scintigraphy performed for suspected metastases shows only mild uptake at a rib fracture. What is the most appropriate interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Myeloma lesions often show little tracer uptake, so scintigraphy underestimates disease extent", "B": "Scintigraphy is the more sensitive test, so the skeletal survey findings are probably false positive", "C": "Lytic metastases from renal carcinoma commonly appear photopenic, favouring metastatic disease over myeloma", "D": "The lesions are likely brown tumours, since hyperparathyroidism produces intense generalised tracer uptake"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Myeloma lesions often show little tracer uptake, so scintigraphy underestimates disease extent): Multiple lytic lesions with anaemia and relatively little uptake on bone scintigraphy are classic for myeloma, where osteoblastic activity is low and the survey is the better test. Renal metastases can be photopenic, but myeloma is more likely with this combination. Brown tumours would normally take up tracer avidly. Assuming scintigraphy is more sensitive would wrongly dismiss the survey findings."}, {"id": 300, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 19-year-old footballer has persistent deep ankle pain months after a sprain. MRI shows a posteromedial talar dome lesion with an osteochondral fragment partly separated from the bone, surrounded by a rim of high T2 signal fluid, with intact overlying cartilage. Which interpretation of stability is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Normal variant, because medial talar dome marrow oedema is common after sprain", "B": "Stable lesion, because the overlying cartilage appears intact on imaging", "C": "Unstable lesion, because high T2 signal fluid undermines the fragment", "D": "Stable lesion, because the fragment remains within its bony crater"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Unstable lesion, because high T2 signal fluid undermines the fragment): A high T2 signal rim or cyst-like fluid beneath an osteochondral fragment indicates instability, as synovial fluid tracks around it, even with intact overlying cartilage. Such lesions often need surgery. Fragment position in the crater alone does not prove stability. Marrow oedema is common but does not account for a separate fragment with underlying fluid."}, {"id": 302, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 24-year-old footballer presents with a knee that will not fully extend after a twisting injury. Sagittal proton density MRI shows a low-signal curved fragment in the intercondylar notch lying parallel and anterior to the posterior cruciate ligament, and the medial meniscus body appears truncated on coronal images. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Complete ACL rupture with the torn stump flipped anteriorly into the intercondylar notch", "B": "Displaced bucket-handle tear of the lateral meniscus with a flipped meniscus sign", "C": "Displaced bucket-handle tear of the medial meniscus with a double PCL sign", "D": "Displaced meniscal flap tear of the medial meniscus with a fragment in the posterior recess"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Displaced bucket-handle tear of the medial meniscus with a double PCL sign): A low-signal curved fragment in the notch lying parallel and anterior to the posterior cruciate ligament, with a truncated meniscal body, is the double PCL sign of a displaced bucket-handle tear. The truncated medial meniscus places it medially. A flipped ACL stump lies in the anterior notch with absent ligament fibres. A flap tear gives a fragment in a recess without the long central body loss."}, {"id": 306, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 16-year-old girl has an acutely swollen, painful knee after her kneecap popped out and relocated spontaneously while she was dancing. MRI is requested to assess the injury. Which combination of findings best supports transient lateral patellar dislocation rather than an ACL injury?", "options": {"A": "Marrow oedema in the anterior tibia and posterior femoral condyle from a dashboard injury", "B": "Oedema in the mid lateral femoral condyle and posterolateral tibial plateau with an ACL tear", "C": "Marrow oedema in the inferomedial patella and anterolateral femoral condyle with a torn medial patellofemoral ligament", "D": "Oedema in the medial femoral condyle and medial tibial plateau with a meniscal root tear"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Marrow oedema in the inferomedial patella and anterolateral femoral condyle with a torn medial patellofemoral ligament): As the patella dislocates laterally and relocates, the inferomedial patella strikes the anterolateral femoral condyle and the medial patellofemoral ligament tears. The mid lateral femoral condyle and posterolateral tibial plateau pattern belongs to ACL rupture. The anterior tibia and posterior femoral pattern suggests a posterior force injury, and the medial compartment pattern suggests a degenerative root problem."}, {"id": 314, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 34-year-old woman who works at a desk has anterior hip pain after prolonged sitting and on deep flexion. A well-centred, weight-bearing AP pelvis radiograph is reviewed for signs of acetabular overcoverage and retroversion. Which finding best supports pincer-type femoroacetabular impingement caused by acetabular retroversion?", "options": {"A": "Crossover sign with a prominent ischial spine and posterior wall sign", "B": "Pistol-grip deformity of the proximal femur with an anterior head-neck bump", "C": "Alpha angle above 55 degrees on a cross-table lateral view", "D": "Tonnis angle above 10 degrees with a lateral centre-edge angle under 20"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Crossover sign with a prominent ischial spine and posterior wall sign): Acetabular retroversion shows the crossover sign, a prominent ischial spine and a posterior wall sign. An alpha angle above 55 degrees and a pistol-grip deformity describe cam morphology on the femoral side. A raised Tonnis angle with a low centre-edge angle indicates dysplasia and undercoverage rather than overcoverage."}, {"id": 318, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 55-year-old overweight woman has gradual medial ankle swelling and a progressively flattening arch. MRI shows an enlarged tendon behind the medial malleolus with a longitudinal split, surrounding fluid, and thinning of the spring ligament. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Flexor digitorum longus tenosynovitis with a tendon tear at the knot of Henry", "B": "Deltoid ligament insufficiency with medial talar tilt and valgus ankle", "C": "Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction with acquired flatfoot deformity", "D": "Tarsal tunnel syndrome from a ganglion compressing the tibial nerve"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction with acquired flatfoot deformity): A split, enlarged tendon behind the medial malleolus with surrounding fluid and a thinned spring ligament in a middle-aged woman with a progressive flat arch fits posterior tibial tendon dysfunction. Flexor digitorum longus disease is rare and does not flatten the arch. Tarsal tunnel syndrome gives neuropathic symptoms. Deltoid insufficiency causes valgus tilt but not a tendon split."}, {"id": 319, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 30-year-old man has ulnar-sided wrist pain after falling onto a pronated hand. MRI shows fluid signal at the ulnar attachment of the triangular fibrocartilage complex at the fovea, with an associated ulnar styloid base fracture. The central disc is intact. Which Palmer category best fits this injury?", "options": {"A": "Class 1B traumatic avulsion at the ulnar attachment of the complex", "B": "Class 1A central perforation of the articular disc", "C": "Class 1D traumatic detachment from the radial attachment at the sigmoid notch", "D": "Class 2C degenerative perforation with ulnocarpal impaction and lunate chondromalacia"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Class 1B traumatic avulsion at the ulnar attachment of the complex): Ulnar-sided peripheral detachment at the fovea, often with a styloid fracture, is Palmer 1B. A 1A tear is a central disc perforation. A 1D injury detaches the radial attachment. Class 2C is a degenerative perforation seen with ulnar impaction and lunate change."}, {"id": 330, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 45-year-old man has right foot drop and weak great toe extension, with numbness over the dorsum of the foot. MRI shows a right paracentral posterolateral extrusion at L4/5 that fills the lateral recess and displaces the thecal sac. Which nerve root is most likely compressed?", "options": {"A": "Right L5 traversing root within the lateral recess", "B": "Right L4 exiting root within the neural foramen", "C": "Right S1 traversing root at the L5/S1 lateral recess", "D": "Cauda equina roots in the central canal bilaterally"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Right L5 traversing root within the lateral recess): A paracentral or posterolateral herniation at L4/5 compresses the traversing L5 root, which explains the foot drop and dorsal foot sensory loss. The L4 exiting root is affected by foraminal herniation. The S1 root is affected at L5/S1. Bilateral cauda equina compression would produce symmetrical findings."}, {"id": 339, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 58-year-old diabetic man has fever and severe lumbar pain for two weeks, and his plain radiographs are normal. MRI is performed to separate pyogenic discitis-osteomyelitis from Modic type 1 degenerative endplate change. Which MRI finding most supports infection rather than degeneration?", "options": {"A": "Disc desiccation with a vacuum phenomenon and endplate osteophytes", "B": "T2 hyperintense disc without an intranuclear cleft and an enhancing paravertebral collection", "C": "Schmorl node with a focal sclerotic rim at the superior endplate", "D": "Endplate marrow oedema with a preserved low-signal degenerated disc"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (T2 hyperintense disc without an intranuclear cleft and an enhancing paravertebral collection): Infected discs are bright on T2, lose the intranuclear cleft, and are accompanied by endplate erosion and soft tissue collections. Vacuum phenomenon and desiccation point to degeneration. Modic 1 changes keep a dark disc. A Schmorl node is a benign herniation."}, {"id": 340, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 26-year-old HLA-B27 positive man has had two years of inflammatory back pain that improves with exercise, and plain pelvic radiographs are normal. MRI of the sacroiliac joints is requested to look for active inflammation. Which MRI finding best defines active sacroiliitis?", "options": {"A": "Periarticular marrow oedema on STIR seen on at least two slices", "B": "Joint space widening with erosions and a normal marrow signal", "C": "Subchondral sclerosis seen as low signal on all sequences", "D": "Subchondral fatty metaplasia with bright T1 signal and no oedema"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Periarticular marrow oedema on STIR seen on at least two slices): Active inflammation appears as bone marrow oedema on STIR or fat-saturated T2 near the joint on multiple slices. Fatty metaplasia and sclerosis represent chronic structural damage. Joint space widening and erosions show structural change rather than activity."}, {"id": 341, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 60-year-old man has stiffness but little pain. A thoracic spine radiograph shows thick flowing ossification along the anterolateral right side of five contiguous vertebrae, with preserved disc heights. Both sacroiliac joints are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Ankylosing spondylitis with bamboo spine and symmetrical sacroiliac fusion", "B": "Diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis (DISH) affecting the thoracic spine", "C": "Psoriatic spondyloarthropathy with asymmetric non-marginal syndesmophytes and sacroiliitis", "D": "Degenerative spondylosis deformans with disc space narrowing and endplate osteophytes"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis (DISH) affecting the thoracic spine): Flowing ossification across four or more levels with preserved discs and normal sacroiliac joints defines DISH, usually right-sided in the thoracic spine. Ankylosing spondylitis produces thin syndesmophytes with sacroiliitis. Psoriatic disease shows non-marginal syndesmophytes. Spondylosis deformans narrows the disc."}, {"id": 342, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 44-year-old man with long-standing psoriasis has back pain and stiffness, and radiographs of the spine and pelvis are reviewed for the pattern of disease. Which radiographic pattern would favour psoriatic or reactive spondyloarthropathy rather than classic ankylosing spondylitis in this man?", "options": {"A": "Asymmetric unilateral sacroiliitis with bulky non-marginal syndesmophytes in the lumbar spine", "B": "Diffuse ankylosis with a continuous bamboo spine from the sacrum upward", "C": "Bilateral sacroiliac fusion with shiny corners and squared vertebral bodies", "D": "Symmetric bilateral sacroiliitis with thin marginal syndesmophytes and early squaring"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Asymmetric unilateral sacroiliitis with bulky non-marginal syndesmophytes in the lumbar spine): Psoriatic and reactive disease tend to be asymmetric with bulky non-marginal syndesmophytes. Symmetric sacroiliitis with thin marginal syndesmophytes, shiny corners, squaring and bamboo spine belong to classic ankylosing spondylitis."}, {"id": 343, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 48-year-old man has heel pain on the first steps in the morning, easing with walking. Ultrasound shows the plantar fascia measuring 6 mm at the calcaneal insertion with hypoechoic change. Radiographs show a small inferior calcaneal spur. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Calcaneal stress fracture with marrow oedema on MRI", "B": "Partial fascial tear with perifascial fluid after trauma", "C": "Plantar fibromatosis with a discrete nodule in the fascia", "D": "Plantar fasciitis with thickening above the 4 mm threshold"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Plantar fasciitis with thickening above the 4 mm threshold): A fascia measuring about 6 mm at the calcaneal insertion with hypoechoic change and a small spur is the typical appearance of plantar fasciitis, and the morning first-step pain fits. Plantar fibromatosis produces a discrete nodule, usually in the mid-arch rather than at the insertion. A stress fracture needs marrow oedema or a cortical abnormality on cross-sectional imaging. A partial tear follows a sudden event and shows perifascial fluid with interruption of fibres."}, {"id": 351, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 68-year-old man presents three hours after sudden right hemiparesis and aphasia. Non-contrast CT shows a hyperdense left M1 segment and ASPECTS of 9. CT perfusion shows a Tmax greater than 6 seconds volume of 120 mL and a cerebral blood volume core of 15 mL. Which interpretation is most appropriate for treatment planning?", "options": {"A": "Large salvageable penumbra around a small core, favouring urgent endovascular clot retrieval", "B": "Extensive mismatch, favouring supportive care and repeat imaging at 24 hours", "C": "Salvageable penumbra, favouring intravenous thrombolysis alone for the proximal occlusion", "D": "Core volume above the threshold for benefit, so reperfusion is unlikely to help"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Large salvageable penumbra around a small core, favouring urgent endovascular clot retrieval): A Tmax abnormality of 120 mL against a core of 15 mL shows a large volume of tissue at risk, and the dense M1 segment is a target for retrieval at three hours. Intravenous thrombolysis alone is less likely to recanalise a proximal M1 occlusion than retrieval. A core of 15 mL is well below the size at which reperfusion stops helping. Waiting for repeat imaging would let the penumbra infarct."}, {"id": 363, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 27-year-old woman using oral contraception has two weeks of progressive headache, then a generalised seizure. Non-contrast CT shows a hyperdense superior sagittal sinus and bilateral parasagittal frontal haemorrhagic lesions that do not follow arterial territories. Which test best confirms the diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "MRI diffusion sequences to show an arterial territory infarct", "B": "CT angiography of the circle of Willis to exclude vasculitis", "C": "Contrast MRI of the brain to exclude haemorrhagic metastasis", "D": "Venography by CT or MR showing a filling defect in the sinus"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Venography by CT or MR showing a filling defect in the sinus): A hyperdense sinus with bilateral parasagittal haemorrhage in a woman on hormones points to cerebral venous thrombosis, which is confirmed by a filling defect on CT or MR venography. Diffusion imaging would show infarction in an arterial pattern, which these lesions do not follow. CT angiography of the arteries cannot show sinus occlusion. Contrast MRI may show some change but does not confirm thrombus."}, {"id": 365, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 66-year-old immunocompetent man has subacute confusion. CT shows a hyperdense periventricular mass. MRI shows a homogeneously enhancing lesion crossing the splenium of the corpus callosum, with restricted diffusion, relatively low perfusion, and mild surrounding oedema. No necrosis is present. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Solitary metastasis from a lung adenocarcinoma presenting without a known primary", "B": "Primary B cell lymphoma confined to the central nervous system", "C": "Glioblastoma with an unusually solid and non-necrotic growth pattern", "D": "Tumefactive demyelination presenting with a rare homogeneous enhancement pattern"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Primary B cell lymphoma confined to the central nervous system): Dense cellular tumours are hyperattenuating on CT, restrict diffusion and enhance uniformly, typical for lymphoma in the periventricular white matter. Glioblastoma usually necrotic with raised perfusion. A metastasis is normally spherical with greater oedema. Tumefactive demyelination produces an open ring and less restriction."}, {"id": 369, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 33-year-old man has headache and ataxia. MRI shows a cystic right cerebellar lesion with an avidly enhancing 1 cm mural nodule and adjacent flow voids. He has a history of bilateral retinal vascular lesions. Which condition does this combination best suggest?", "options": {"A": "Tuberous sclerosis with a subependymal giant cell astrocytoma at the foramen", "B": "Neurofibromatosis type 2 with multiple intracranial schwannomas and meningiomas", "C": "Von Hippel Lindau disease causing a cerebellar haemangioblastoma", "D": "Sturge Weber syndrome with pial angiomatosis and cortical calcification"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Von Hippel Lindau disease causing a cerebellar haemangioblastoma): An enhancing nodule with vascular flow voids in a cerebellar cyst is typical of haemangioblastoma, and retinal angiomas complete the picture of Von Hippel Lindau. Neurofibromatosis type 2 gives bilateral vestibular schwannomas and meningiomas. Tuberous sclerosis tumours arise near the foramen of Monro. Sturge Weber has leptomeningeal angiomas."}, {"id": 370, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 31-year-old woman has subacute right arm weakness. MRI shows a 3 cm left frontal white matter lesion with mild mass effect and incomplete ring enhancement, the open portion facing the cortex. Perfusion shows low relative cerebral blood volume. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Tumefactive demyelination, usually in the setting of multiple sclerosis", "B": "Cerebral abscess with a developing capsule and surrounding oedema", "C": "High grade astrocytoma with an incomplete necrotic rim and mass effect", "D": "Resolving subacute infarct with luxury perfusion in a vascular territory"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tumefactive demyelination, usually in the setting of multiple sclerosis): An open ring that opens toward the cortex, with low perfusion and modest mass effect, strongly suggests active demyelination. A high grade glioma would show elevated blood volume. An abscess forms a complete ring thicker on the cortical side. An infarct would follow a vascular territory and not present subacutely in this way."}, {"id": 372, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 58-year-old man has fever, behavioural change and a focal seizure. MRI shows asymmetric T2 hyperintensity and restricted diffusion in the right medial temporal lobe, insula and inferior frontal gyrus, with sparing of the basal ganglia. Which diagnosis is most likely, and what should follow?", "options": {"A": "Right middle cerebral artery infarct, with thrombolysis if within the time window", "B": "Creutzfeldt Jakob disease, with electroencephalography and 14-3-3 testing as priority", "C": "Limbic encephalitis from paraneoplastic antibodies, with immediate immunotherapy", "D": "Herpes simplex encephalitis, treated with empirical intravenous aciclovir at once"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Herpes simplex encephalitis, treated with empirical intravenous aciclovir at once): Fever, seizure and limbic plus insular involvement with basal ganglia sparing is classic for herpes encephalitis, where treatment is started without waiting for confirmation. An MCA infarct would involve the putamen and follow a territory. Paraneoplastic limbic disease is subacute and usually bilateral. Prion disease has a slower course and cortical ribboning."}, {"id": 374, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 39-year-old man with advanced HIV infection and a CD4 count of 40 has headache and fever. MRI shows multiple ring enhancing lesions in the basal ganglia and corticomedullary junction, one with an eccentric enhancing nodule within the ring. Which additional finding best favours toxoplasmosis over lymphoma?", "options": {"A": "Intense uptake in the lesions on fluorodeoxyglucose positron emission tomography", "B": "Positive Epstein Barr virus polymerase chain reaction in cerebrospinal fluid", "C": "No tracer uptake in the lesions on thallium-201 single photon emission imaging", "D": "Single periventricular lesion crossing the corpus callosum with subependymal spread"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (No tracer uptake in the lesions on thallium-201 single photon emission imaging): Toxoplasma abscesses are not metabolically active and show no thallium uptake, while lymphoma is avid. Multiple basal ganglia lesions with an eccentric target sign also favour infection. A solitary callosal mass, intense glucose uptake and Epstein Barr virus DNA in cerebrospinal fluid each point toward lymphoma."}, {"id": 375, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 63-year-old woman has a four month history of rapidly progressive dementia, myoclonus and gait disturbance. Electroencephalography shows periodic sharp wave complexes and cerebrospinal fluid protein is mildly raised with few cells. Diffusion weighted MRI is most likely to show which pattern?", "options": {"A": "Cortical ribbon restriction together with caudate and putamen signal change", "B": "Bilateral symmetrical restricted diffusion limited to the hippocampi and amygdalae", "C": "Multifocal restricted diffusion in watershed territories between arterial supplies", "D": "Confluent periventricular white matter restriction sparing the cortex and basal ganglia"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cortical ribbon restriction together with caudate and putamen signal change): Sporadic prion disease produces cortical gyral restriction on diffusion plus basal ganglia involvement, making diffusion the most sensitive imaging sequence. A limbic restricted pattern suggests autoimmune or herpes encephalitis. Watershed lesions arise from hypotension. White matter predominant restriction suggests a leukoencephalopathy."}, {"id": 384, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 9-year-old boy has headaches, growth failure and bitemporal visual field loss. CT shows a suprasellar mass with a large cystic component, coarse calcification and a solid enhancing portion. MRI shows the cyst is T1 hyperintense. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Optic pathway glioma associated with neurofibromatosis type 1 and chiasmal expansion", "B": "Adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma arising from Rathke pouch epithelial remnants", "C": "Germinoma of the infundibulum presenting with polyuria and diabetes insipidus", "D": "Pituitary macroadenoma with cystic degeneration and suprasellar extension in a child"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma arising from Rathke pouch epithelial remnants): A calcified, cystic and solid suprasellar mass in a child, with oily cyst fluid causing T1 hyperintensity, is typical of the adamantinomatous type. Adenomas are rare in children and seldom calcify. Optic pathway glioma expands the nerve and chiasm. Germinoma usually thickens the stalk and causes diabetes insipidus."}, {"id": 389, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A term neonate who had a uterine rupture and severe acidosis at birth has seizures on day one. MRI on day three shows restricted diffusion and T1 hyperintensity in the ventrolateral thalami, posterior putamina, perirolandic cortex and brainstem. Which injury pattern does this represent?", "options": {"A": "Profound acute hypoxic ischaemic injury of the metabolically active deep nuclei", "B": "Kernicterus with bilirubin deposition in the globus pallidus and subthalamic nuclei", "C": "Prolonged partial hypoxia injuring the watershed cortex and white matter", "D": "Neonatal hypoglycaemia with parieto-occipital cortical and white matter injury"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Profound acute hypoxic ischaemic injury of the metabolically active deep nuclei): A sudden profound insult at term injures the most metabolically active regions: the ventrolateral thalami, posterior putamina, perirolandic cortex and brainstem. Prolonged partial hypoxia instead involves the watershed cortex and white matter. Hypoglycaemia mainly injures the occipital and parietal cortex. Kernicterus also gives T1 hyperintensity but centres on the globus pallidus and subthalamic nuclei."}, {"id": 391, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 35-year-old woman has episodic severe headaches that worsen on bending forward and ease when lying down, with one syncopal episode. CT shows a 1.5 cm round hyperdense lesion at the anterosuperior third ventricle with mild enlargement of both lateral ventricles. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Colloid cyst intermittently obstructing flow through the foramina of Monro", "B": "Choroid plexus papilloma within the atrium of the lateral ventricle", "C": "Central neurocytoma attached to the septum pellucidum with calcified clusters", "D": "Subependymal giant cell astrocytoma at the foramen of Monro"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Colloid cyst intermittently obstructing flow through the foramina of Monro): A smooth hyperdense round lesion at the anterior third ventricle roof with positional headache is typical for a colloid cyst, which acts as a ball valve at the foramina of Monro. Central neurocytoma is a solid lobulated mass on the septum. Giant cell astrocytoma needs the syndrome. A papilloma sits in the atrium or fourth ventricle."}, {"id": 392, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 28-year-old woman has occipital headache on coughing and dissociated sensory loss in both hands. MRI shows cerebellar tonsils extending 11 mm below the foramen magnum with a peg-like shape and a long cervical cord cavity. What additional imaging assessment is most useful before surgical planning?", "options": {"A": "Contrast MRI of the whole spine to exclude intramedullary tumour", "B": "MR venography to exclude venous sinus stenosis as a secondary cause", "C": "Cine MRI of cerebrospinal fluid flow across the craniocervical junction", "D": "Flexion and extension radiographs to assess craniocervical instability"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cine MRI of cerebrospinal fluid flow across the craniocervical junction): Tonsillar descent with a syrinx indicates Chiari I, and cine phase contrast MRI assesses cerebrospinal fluid flow across the foramen magnum to support decompression. Whole spine contrast MRI is reasonable only if the syrinx looks atypical. Flexion extension films assess instability, which is not the main planning question. Venography addresses a secondary cause that is not suggested here."}, {"id": 396, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 72-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes has severe otalgia and purulent otorrhoea for three weeks unresponsive to topical drops. CT shows soft tissue in the external auditory canal, erosion of the floor of the canal and obliteration of fat below the skull base. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Keratosis obturans with canal widening and medial keratin plug", "B": "Acute coalescent mastoiditis with a subperiosteal abscess behind the ear", "C": "Necrotising otitis externa with early skull base osteomyelitis", "D": "Squamous cell carcinoma of the external auditory canal with bone invasion"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Necrotising otitis externa with early skull base osteomyelitis): A diabetic or immunosuppressed older patient with severe pain, canal floor erosion and loss of the fat planes at the skull base has necrotising otitis externa. Carcinoma can look similar and may need biopsy, but the clinical picture favours infection. Keratosis obturans and mastoiditis do not produce this skull base pattern."}, {"id": 401, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 6-year-old child with ethmoid sinusitis has medial eyelid swelling, proptosis and pain on eye movement. Contrast CT shows a lentiform rim-enhancing collection along the medial orbital wall displacing the medial rectus. What is the most likely diagnosis and management?", "options": {"A": "Orbital cellulitis without a collection, treated with antibiotics", "B": "Preseptal cellulitis, treated with oral antibiotics and review", "C": "Subperiosteal abscess needing urgent surgical review for drainage", "D": "Cavernous sinus thrombosis needing both anticoagulation and antibiotics"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Subperiosteal abscess needing urgent surgical review for drainage): A lentiform rim-enhancing collection along the medial wall displacing the medial rectus after ethmoid sinusitis is a subperiosteal abscess, which usually needs surgical drainage in a child with proptosis. Orbital cellulitis without a collection is treated medically. Preseptal cellulitis lies anterior to the septum and spares movement. Cavernous sinus thrombosis shows a filling defect and bilateral signs rather than a medial collection."}, {"id": 402, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 45-year-old woman with Graves disease has bilateral proptosis and lid retraction, with mild discomfort on eye movement and recent weight loss. CT of the orbits shows bilateral enlargement of the inferior and medial rectus muscle bellies. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Idiopathic orbital myositis with tendon thickening and adjacent fat stranding", "B": "Thyroid-associated orbitopathy sparing the tendons of the enlarged muscles", "C": "Carotid-cavernous fistula with a dilated superior ophthalmic vein", "D": "IgG4-related disease with lacrimal gland and infraorbital nerve enlargement"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Thyroid-associated orbitopathy sparing the tendons of the enlarged muscles): Bilateral enlargement of the inferior and medial rectus bellies with tendon sparing in a patient with Graves disease is thyroid orbitopathy. Idiopathic myositis involves the tendon and is painful. IgG4-related disease enlarges the lacrimal gland and infraorbital nerve and does not follow the thyroid history. A carotid-cavernous fistula shows a dilated superior ophthalmic vein, not enlarged muscle bellies."}, {"id": 405, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 3-year-old child has fever, neck stiffness and drooling. Contrast CT of the neck shows a retropharyngeal low-density collection with straightening of the cervical lordosis, expanding the prevertebral soft tissues to more than the vertebral body width. Which finding best distinguishes abscess from reactive oedema?", "options": {"A": "Low attenuation of the collection relative to muscle", "B": "Anterior bowing of the posterior pharyngeal wall and airway", "C": "Enlarged reactive jugulodigastric nodes with preserved fatty hila", "D": "A thick enhancing rim around the low-density collection"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A thick enhancing rim around the low-density collection): Rim enhancement around a low-density collection indicates a drainable abscess, whereas reactive oedema has no enhancing wall. Low attenuation and airway narrowing occur with both oedema and abscess, so neither discriminates. Reactive nodes with preserved hila accompany both conditions. A thick enhancing wall is the feature that separates the two."}, {"id": 408, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 48-year-old woman has a painless slowly enlarging lump in the superficial lobe of the parotid gland, present for two years with no facial weakness. MRI shows a well-circumscribed lobulated mass with markedly high T2 signal, a thin low signal capsule and homogeneous enhancement. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Pleomorphic adenoma with a thin low signal capsule", "B": "Warthin tumour with cystic and solid components in an older smoker", "C": "Mucoepidermoid carcinoma of low grade with a poorly defined edge", "D": "Parotid lymphoma presenting in a patient with Sjogren syndrome"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Pleomorphic adenoma with a thin low signal capsule): A well-defined lobulated parotid mass with very high T2 signal and a thin capsule is typical of pleomorphic adenoma, the commonest salivary tumour. Warthin tumour favours older male smokers and may be bilateral. Carcinomas are infiltrative and lymphoma arises in glands with chronic autoimmune disease."}, {"id": 412, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 40-year-old woman has a 17 mm thyroid nodule found on ultrasound during assessment of a neck swelling. It is solid, markedly hypoechoic, taller than wide and contains punctate echogenic foci. Using ACR TI-RADS, which management is most appropriate for this nodule?", "options": {"A": "No further action because the nodule is solid without calcification", "B": "Follow-up ultrasound in one year because the nodule is under 20 mm", "C": "Fine needle aspiration, since the nodule is TR5 and exceeds 10 mm", "D": "Radioiodine scan to assess whether the nodule is functioning"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Fine needle aspiration, since the nodule is TR5 and exceeds 10 mm): Solid composition, marked hypoechogenicity, a taller-than-wide shape and punctate echogenic foci place the nodule in TR5 on ACR TI-RADS, where biopsy is recommended at 10 mm or more. One year follow-up applies to lower categories, and radioiodine scanning is for hyperthyroidism."}, {"id": 416, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 62-year-old man with hoarseness has a glottic tumour on CT and MRI. The tumour infiltrates the paraglottic space with fixation of the vocal cord, but the thyroid cartilage is intact. Under current TNM staging, how is the primary tumour classified?", "options": {"A": "T2, because of extension to the supraglottis with normal mobility", "B": "T4a, because of any thyroid cartilage involvement in the tumour", "C": "T1b, because both cords are involved without cord fixation", "D": "T3, because of cord fixation and paraglottic space invasion"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (T3, because of cord fixation and paraglottic space invasion): Vocal cord fixation or paraglottic space invasion without cartilage penetration is T3 in a glottic cancer. T2 requires extension to supraglottis or subglottis, or impaired mobility. T4a needs invasion through the outer cortex of thyroid cartilage or into extralaryngeal tissue."}, {"id": 418, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 38-year-old man of southern Chinese origin has unilateral serous otitis media and an upper neck node. MRI shows an enhancing nasopharyngeal mass centred on the right posterolateral wall, extending into the parapharyngeal space and the skull base marrow. Which diagnosis fits best?", "options": {"A": "Nasopharyngeal carcinoma with parapharyngeal and skull base invasion", "B": "Adenoid cystic carcinoma with perineural spread to the skull base", "C": "Lymphoma with bulky homogeneous infiltration of the nasopharynx", "D": "Chordoma centred on the clivus with a lobulated T2 hyperintense mass"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Nasopharyngeal carcinoma with parapharyngeal and skull base invasion): A unilateral enhancing nasopharyngeal mass with serous otitis media, parapharyngeal extension and clival marrow involvement in a man from an endemic region is nasopharyngeal carcinoma. Lymphoma is bulkier with homogeneous signal and less invasion. A chordoma arises in the clivus itself with very bright T2 signal. Adenoid cystic carcinoma tracks along nerves and is rare in this site."}, {"id": 419, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 56-year-old non-smoking man has a painless neck mass noticed for six weeks. CT shows a 3 cm cystic level II node with a thin enhancing wall, and an enhancing 15 mm lesion in the right palatine tonsil. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Tuberculous cervical lymphadenitis from caseating necrosis in the node", "B": "HPV-related oropharyngeal squamous cell carcinoma with cystic node", "C": "Second branchial cleft cyst in a middle-aged adult", "D": "Thyroglossal duct cyst arising near the hyoid bone"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (HPV-related oropharyngeal squamous cell carcinoma with cystic node): A cystic level II node in an adult, together with a small tonsillar primary, strongly suggests HPV-related oropharyngeal cancer with cystic nodal metastasis. A second branchial cleft cyst can look similar, but the tonsillar lesion makes it unlikely. Tuberculous nodes and thyroglossal cysts do not match."}, {"id": 420, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 49-year-old man with a supraglottic laryngeal primary has a node posterior to the internal jugular vein at the level of the hyoid bone, separated from the vein by a fat plane. Which level and clinical implication are correct for this node?", "options": {"A": "Level VIIA, a retropharyngeal group not accessible at standard dissection", "B": "Level IIB, a node that can be spared in selected neck dissections", "C": "Level IB, a submandibular group draining the floor of the mouth", "D": "Level IIA, which is removed with a modified radical neck dissection"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Level IIB, a node that can be spared in selected neck dissections): Level II nodes lie from the skull base to the hyoid; IIB lies posterior to the internal jugular vein with a fat plane. This sublevel may be spared in selected dissections where it is not at risk. Level IIA is anterior or in contact with the vein, and the others are in different regions."}, {"id": 427, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 30-year-old man struck by a cricket ball has double vision on upgaze. CT shows a defect in the orbital floor with herniation of the inferior rectus and fat into the maxillary sinus. Which complication is most relevant to examine for?", "options": {"A": "Retrobulbar haemorrhage causing a tense proptosed globe and visual loss", "B": "Entrapment of the inferior rectus muscle with restricted upgaze", "C": "Cavernous sinus thrombosis with progressive painful eye movement loss", "D": "Optic nerve avulsion with an afferent pupillary defect and visual loss"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Entrapment of the inferior rectus muscle with restricted upgaze): Herniation of the inferior rectus through an orbital floor fracture can trap the muscle and restrict upgaze, causing diplopia. Optic nerve avulsion, cavernous sinus thrombosis and retrobulbar haemorrhage have different clinical findings."}, {"id": 429, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 28-year-old man has a lucent mandibular lesion in the angle and ramus with a scalloped, corticated border. It extends along the long axis of the mandible with minimal bony expansion and does not displace a tooth crown or attach to one. Which lesion is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Radicular cyst at the apex of a non-vital tooth", "B": "Odontogenic keratocyst growing along the long axis of the jaw", "C": "Dentigerous cyst attached to the crown of an unerupted tooth", "D": "Ameloblastoma with marked buccal cortical expansion in a multilocular lesion"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Odontogenic keratocyst growing along the long axis of the jaw): A lucent angle and ramus lesion with scalloped corticated margins that grows along the long axis with little expansion is typical of odontogenic keratocyst. Dentigerous cysts surround an unerupted crown, ameloblastoma expands bone, and radicular cysts are at apices of non-vital teeth."}, {"id": 439, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 27-year-old woman has subacute sensory symptoms in the legs with a mild gait disturbance. Cervical and thoracic MRI show several T2 hyperintense cord lesions, and brain MRI is awaited. Which pattern would most support multiple sclerosis rather than neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder?", "options": {"A": "A single lesion spanning more than three contiguous segments and occupying most of the axial cross-section", "B": "Lesion extending into the area postrema and dorsal medulla, with intense patchy enhancement", "C": "Several short lesions, each under two vertebral segments, lying peripherally in the dorsolateral cord", "D": "Central grey matter lesion with a bright spot on axial T2 and associated cord swelling"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Several short lesions, each under two vertebral segments, lying peripherally in the dorsolateral cord): MS cord plaques are typically short, under two vertebral segments, eccentric and dorsolateral, occupying less than half the cross-section. NMOSD produces longitudinally extensive, central lesions, often with an axial bright spot and with area postrema or dorsal medullary involvement."}, {"id": 442, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 61-year-old man develops sudden bilateral leg weakness with loss of pain and temperature sensation but preserved vibration sense after aortic repair. Axial T2 at the cervicothoracic junction shows paired symmetrical hyperintensities in the ventral grey matter of the cord, with diffusion restriction. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Spinal dural arteriovenous fistula, with central cord oedema and dorsal serpentine flow voids", "B": "Anterior spinal artery territory infarction, producing the owl's eyes appearance of the anterior horns", "C": "Acute transverse myelitis, with a central lesion spanning several segments and patchy enhancement", "D": "Neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder, with a long central cord lesion and patchy enhancement"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Anterior spinal artery territory infarction, producing the owl's eyes appearance of the anterior horns): Sudden bilateral weakness with loss of pain and temperature but preserved vibration after aortic surgery points to the anterior spinal artery territory. Paired ventral grey matter hyperintensities with diffusion restriction give the owl's eyes appearance. Dural fistula and demyelinating or inflammatory myelopathies evolve over days to weeks and do not restrict diffusion in this way."}, {"id": 445, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 34-year-old man is ventilated after a road traffic collision with a severe head and neck injury. Cervical CT shows prevertebral soft tissue swelling and subarachnoid blood at the foramen magnum. Which coronal CT finding would most strongly indicate atlanto-occipital dissociation?", "options": {"A": "Combined overhang of the C1 lateral masses over C2 of more than 7 mm", "B": "Widening of the atlantodental interval to 4 mm, as measured on the sagittal reformat", "C": "Anterior displacement of the C2 vertebral body on C3 exceeding 3 mm at the facets", "D": "Widening of the occipital condyle to C1 joint beyond 2 mm on coronal reformats"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Widening of the occipital condyle to C1 joint beyond 2 mm on coronal reformats): Atlanto-occipital dissociation is a craniocervical ligamentous injury, shown by a condyle to C1 joint space above about 2 mm, a widened basion-dens interval, and prevertebral swelling. Atlantodental widening, lateral mass overhang and C2-C3 translation each indicate injuries at lower levels."}, {"id": 446, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 32-year-old man dives into shallow water and has severe neck pain. CT shows fractures through the anterior and posterior arches of C1 on both sides. Open-mouth reconstruction shows the lateral masses overhang the C2 body by a combined 9 mm. What does this finding indicate?", "options": {"A": "Stable Jefferson fracture with intact ligaments, suitable for rigid collar immobilisation", "B": "Probable transverse atlantal ligament rupture, making this a mechanically unstable injury", "C": "Atlanto-occipital dissociation with a widened basion-dens interval needing occipitocervical fusion", "D": "Type II odontoid fracture with lateral mass displacement, needing halo immobilisation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Probable transverse atlantal ligament rupture, making this a mechanically unstable injury): A two-part burst of the atlas ring is a Jefferson fracture. Combined lateral mass overhang above about 7 mm on the open-mouth view suggests transverse ligament rupture, so the injury is unstable and needs MRI and halo or surgical fixation. Collar treatment suits the stable pattern, while craniocervical dissociation and odontoid fractures have different imaging findings."}, {"id": 447, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 30-year-old man has quadriparesis after a diving injury. Lateral CT reformats show C5 displaced anteriorly on C6 by about half the width of the vertebral body, with an empty facet appearance on both sides. Which injury is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Flexion teardrop fracture, with a triangular anteroinferior corner fragment and retropulsion into the canal", "B": "Unilateral facet dislocation, typically with anterolisthesis of less than a quarter of the vertebral body", "C": "Clay-shoveller fracture of the spinous process, a stable avulsion injury of the lower cervical spine", "D": "Bilateral interfacetal dislocation, an unstable flexion distraction injury with high risk of cord damage"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Bilateral interfacetal dislocation, an unstable flexion distraction injury with high risk of cord damage): Anterolisthesis of about 50 per cent with bilateral perched or locked facets indicates bilateral facet dislocation, a flexion distraction injury with posterior ligament failure and frequent complete cord injury. Unilateral dislocation shows about 25 per cent translation. Teardrop fractures show a corner fragment, and clay-shoveller injuries are stable."}, {"id": 453, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "An 11-year-old boy has a painful left thoracic scoliosis with a Cobb angle of 25 degrees and abnormal abdominal reflexes on examination. Radiographs show no vertebral segmentation anomaly or rib anomaly. He has no family history of scoliosis. Which is the most appropriate next imaging?", "options": {"A": "Whole-spine and craniocervical MRI to look for a syrinx or Chiari malformation", "B": "CT of the thoracic spine to measure vertebral rotation more accurately", "C": "Repeat standing radiographs in six months to monitor curve progression and bracing needs", "D": "Bone scintigraphy to identify a painful vertebral osteoid osteoma as the likely cause"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Whole-spine and craniocervical MRI to look for a syrinx or Chiari malformation): Left thoracic curve, pain, male sex, early onset and abnormal neurological signs are red flags for underlying neural axis pathology such as syringomyelia, Chiari malformation or tethered cord. MRI of the whole spine and craniocervical junction is indicated. Osteoid osteoma is possible but less likely to explain abnormal reflexes."}, {"id": 456, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 57-year-old woman has sudden severe headache with right ptosis, a dilated unreactive pupil and an eye deviated down and out. She has no diabetes and no hypertension. Non-contrast CT of the brain is normal. What is the most appropriate next investigation?", "options": {"A": "CT angiography to look for a posterior communicating artery aneurysm", "B": "Carotid ultrasound to assess for internal carotid stenosis with ocular ischaemia", "C": "MRI of the orbits with fat suppression to assess for orbital pseudotumour", "D": "Contrast MRI of the pituitary to look for apoplexy compressing the cavernous sinus"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (CT angiography to look for a posterior communicating artery aneurysm): A pupil-involving third nerve palsy with acute headache is an aneurysm until proven otherwise, classically the posterior communicating artery at its junction with the internal carotid. CT or catheter angiography is urgent because rupture risk is high. The alternatives do not address the compressive vascular cause."}, {"id": 459, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 33-year-old man with neurofibromatosis type 1 has a known plexiform neurofibroma of the sciatic nerve. Over six months the thigh mass enlarges rapidly, with new pain at rest and night. He has lost weight recently. Which MRI change most suggests malignant transformation?", "options": {"A": "Smooth fusiform enlargement along the nerve with homogeneous enhancement and preserved split-fat sign", "B": "Loss of the target sign with ill-defined margins, heterogeneous enhancement and surrounding oedema", "C": "Large size exceeding five centimetres with a deep thigh location", "D": "Target sign retained in most nodules with increased overall T2 hyperintensity"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Loss of the target sign with ill-defined margins, heterogeneous enhancement and surrounding oedema): In NF1, rapid growth with rest pain suggests malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumour. Loss of the target sign, ill-defined margins, heterogeneous enhancement with necrosis and perilesional oedema favour malignant change. Large deep lesions are at higher risk, but size alone is less specific, and a target sign, smooth margins and split-fat sign favour a benign neurofibroma."}, {"id": 460, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 71-year-old woman presents 3 hours after left hemiparesis and aphasia with an NIHSS score of 18. Non-contrast CT shows an ASPECTS of 9, and CT angiography shows a left M1 middle cerebral artery occlusion. What is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Give intravenous thrombolysis and defer thrombectomy until neurological improvement stops", "B": "Obtain MRI with diffusion-weighted imaging first, because CT cannot reliably define the infarct core", "C": "Proceed directly to thrombectomy, adding intravenous thrombolysis if there is no contraindication", "D": "Delay treatment until CT perfusion confirms a mismatch of at least twenty per cent"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Proceed directly to thrombectomy, adding intravenous thrombolysis if there is no contraindication): A disabling stroke with a small established core and a proximal large vessel occlusion within six hours is a clear indication for thrombectomy. Perfusion imaging is not required in the early window and MRI delays treatment. Thrombolysis should proceed if eligible, but it should not replace or delay thrombectomy."}, {"id": 465, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 41-year-old man develops vertigo and occipital pain a day after a neck manipulation. CT angiography confirms a vertebral artery dissection. He has no connective tissue disease. Which segment of the extracranial vertebral artery is most commonly involved in traumatic dissection of this kind?", "options": {"A": "The V3 segment between the C2 transverse foramen and the foramen magnum", "B": "The V4 segment after dural penetration, as it joins to form the basilar", "C": "The V2 segment passing within the transverse foramina from C6 to C3", "D": "The V1 segment between the subclavian origin and the C6 transverse foramen"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The V3 segment between the C2 transverse foramen and the foramen magnum): The V3 segment is mobile as it loops around the atlas and passes through the dura, so it is vulnerable to rotation and stretch injury. V1 is usually the site of atheroma. V2 is protected within the transverse foramina. V4 dissections are less common but carry a subarachnoid haemorrhage risk."}, {"id": 471, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 46-year-old woman has progressive myelopathy and mediastinal lymphadenopathy. Sagittal MRI shows a long dorsal T2 hyperintense cord segment with linear dorsal subpial enhancement, and axial post-contrast images show enhancement extending to the central canal. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Subacute combined degeneration, where symmetrical dorsal column hyperintensity occurs without enhancement", "B": "Multiple sclerosis, where short peripheral plaques enhance as open rings on contrast", "C": "Spinal dural arteriovenous fistula, where dorsal flow voids accompany central cord oedema", "D": "Spinal cord sarcoidosis, where dorsal subpial and central canal enhancement forms the trident sign"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Spinal cord sarcoidosis, where dorsal subpial and central canal enhancement forms the trident sign): Dorsal subpial enhancement with central canal extension, called the trident sign on axial images, with mediastinal nodes strongly suggests neurosarcoidosis. A dural fistula shows serpentine flow voids, MS shows short peripheral lesions, and B12 myelopathy is non-enhancing and symmetrical."}, {"id": 472, "domain": "neuroradiology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 67-year-old man with prostate cancer has back pain. MRI shows a vertebral metastasis with an epidural component that compresses the thoracic spinal cord, while a thin rim of CSF remains visible around the cord. Using the Bilsky scale, what is the grade?", "options": {"A": "Grade 1c, representing epidural abutment of the cord without cord compression", "B": "Grade 3, representing cord compression with no visible CSF around the cord", "C": "Grade 1b, representing deformation of the thecal sac without contact with the cord", "D": "Grade 2, cord compression with CSF still visible around the cord"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Grade 2, cord compression with CSF still visible around the cord): The Bilsky scale grades epidural spinal cord compression: 1a to 1c are epidural impingement without cord compression, grade 2 is cord compression with CSF visible, and grade 3 is cord compression with no visible CSF. Grades 2 and 3 are high grade and affect decisions about surgery or radiotherapy."}, {"id": 483, "domain": "breast", "question": "A 44-year-old woman with heterogeneously dense breasts has a 2D screening mammogram showing a questionable focal density in the right breast that is not clearly seen on the second view. Tomosynthesis is added and the density resolves on the slice images. Which property of tomosynthesis best explains this?", "options": {"A": "Greater compression force flattens overlapping ducts so they can no longer superimpose on each other", "B": "Thin reconstructed slices separate overlapping fibroglandular tissue and remove a summation artefact", "C": "Intravenous contrast highlights neovascularity so only genuine lesions remain visible on the images", "D": "Higher radiation dose per projection improves contrast resolution of subtle masses within dense tissue"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Thin reconstructed slices separate overlapping fibroglandular tissue and remove a summation artefact): Tomosynthesis acquires projections over an arc and reconstructs thin slices, so overlapping tissue no longer superimposes and apparent masses caused by summation disappear. The technique does not use contrast agents, and dose per view is not the mechanism. Compression is similar to conventional mammography and does not explain the resolution."}, {"id": 485, "domain": "breast", "question": "A 47-year-old woman has a palpable lump. Ultrasound shows a 17 mm hypoechoic solid mass with an oval outline and some microlobulated margin, without calcification or skin involvement. The reader is deciding whether to assign category 4 or 3. Which additional sonographic feature would most strongly increase suspicion for malignancy?", "options": {"A": "Posterior acoustic enhancement behind the mass seen on the same scan plane", "B": "Mild increase in vascularity on colour Doppler within and around the mass", "C": "Non-parallel orientation, with the long axis perpendicular to the skin surface", "D": "Elastography showing intermediate stiffness compared with surrounding fatty tissue"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Non-parallel orientation, with the long axis perpendicular to the skin surface): Non-parallel orientation, a taller-than-wide shape, is among the most predictive sonographic signs of malignancy, because invasive tumours grow across tissue planes. Colour Doppler vascularity and elastography are adjuncts that do not alter the BI-RADS category reliably. Posterior enhancement favours a benign mass such as a fibroadenoma or cyst."}, {"id": 488, "domain": "breast", "question": "A 49-year-old woman has biopsy-proven high-grade DCIS in the upper outer right breast presenting as calcifications. Preoperative MRI shows non-mass enhancement extending from the lesion toward the nipple in a triangular pattern. Which distribution and internal pattern are most typical of DCIS in the BI-RADS MRI lexicon?", "options": {"A": "Diffuse distribution with homogeneous internal enhancement affecting both breasts symmetrically on the study", "B": "Regional distribution with heterogeneous patchy enhancement in the contralateral breast without ductal orientation", "C": "Multiple regions with stippled punctate enhancement consistent with fibroglandular background parenchymal enhancement", "D": "Segmental distribution with clustered ring or clumped internal enhancement"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Segmental distribution with clustered ring or clumped internal enhancement): Segmental or linear non-mass enhancement follows a duct system, with a clumped or clustered ring internal pattern being the most specific for DCIS. Diffuse, symmetric homogeneous enhancement indicates hormonal or physiological change. Regional heterogeneous and stippled patterns are common in benign proliferative change and background parenchymal enhancement."}, {"id": 493, "domain": "breast", "question": "A 74-year-old woman has a new 28 mm circumscribed lobulated breast mass. Ultrasound shows an isoechoic mass with posterior enhancement. On MRI the mass is markedly hyperintense on T2 with no dark internal septations and shows peripheral enhancement progressing inward on later phases. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Epidermal inclusion cyst with keratinous debris and a thin enhancing wall of squamous epithelium", "B": "Pure mucinous carcinoma, with tumour cells suspended in extracellular mucin pools", "C": "Phyllodes tumour with cystic clefts and rapid enlargement over a period of several months", "D": "Fibroadenoma with myxoid change, usually showing non-enhancing internal septations on contrast MRI"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pure mucinous carcinoma, with tumour cells suspended in extracellular mucin pools): Mucin accounts for the marked T2 hyperintensity, and the circumscribed margin with posterior enhancement can mimic a benign lesion. Centripetal progressive enhancement and absence of septations point toward mucinous carcinoma in an older woman. Fibroadenoma is the closest mimic but typically shows dark septations, while phyllodes tumour and inclusion cyst have different demographics and architecture."}, {"id": 495, "domain": "breast", "question": "A 52-year-old woman had stereotactic vacuum-assisted biopsy of a 9 mm cluster of fine pleomorphic calcifications. Specimen radiography confirmed calcifications, and pathology reports pleomorphic lobular carcinoma in situ with comedo-type necrosis, calcifications present in the cores. The result is concordant. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Surveillance only, as lobular neoplasia is a risk marker without any potential for upgrade", "B": "Surgical excision, because the pleomorphic type carries a risk of upgrade and unsampled invasion", "C": "Repeat core biopsy at six months with clip retrieval to track the biopsied site", "D": "Chemoprevention with tamoxifen and annual imaging, avoiding any surgical intervention at this stage"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Surgical excision, because the pleomorphic type carries a risk of upgrade and unsampled invasion): Classic LCIS is usually managed with surveillance, but the pleomorphic variant, especially with necrosis and calcification, behaves more like high-grade DCIS and carries an upgrade risk, so excision is recommended. Chemoprevention reduces risk but does not address possible unsampled invasive disease. Repeat biopsy does not resolve it."}, {"id": 498, "domain": "breast", "question": "A 52-year-old woman with invasive ductal carcinoma has an axillary lymph node assessed by ultrasound. The node measures 14 mm in length, with a preserved fatty hilum and central vascularity. Its cortex is 3 mm thick except where one part of the cortex expands to 6 mm and indents the central hilum. Which feature most specifically suggests metastasis?", "options": {"A": "Focal eccentric cortical lobulation with local effacement of the fatty hilum", "B": "Cortical thickness of 3 mm over most of the node circumference", "C": "Overall node enlargement to 14 mm in its long axis", "D": "Mild increase in colour Doppler vascularity with the fatty hilum remaining visible"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Focal eccentric cortical lobulation with local effacement of the fatty hilum): Focal eccentric cortical expansion with indentation of the fatty hilum suggests a tumour deposit within the cortex. Overall length, mild Doppler vascularity and a thin cortex over most of the node are normal or reactive findings. Only the focal asymmetric thickening is specific for metastasis."}, {"id": 503, "domain": "breast", "question": "A 24-year-old man who is a gym attendee using anabolic steroids has bilateral tender subareolar swelling. Mammography shows a fan-shaped retroareolar density that is symmetric and merges with surrounding fat, without spiculation or calcification. What is the most likely diagnosis and appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Pseudogynaecomastia, requiring dietary advice and repeat imaging at six months", "B": "Lymphoma of the breast, requiring core biopsy and flow cytometry for confirmation", "C": "Gynaecomastia, which needs no biopsy and is managed by removing the cause", "D": "Male breast carcinoma, requiring urgent ultrasound-guided biopsy and staging imaging"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Gynaecomastia, which needs no biopsy and is managed by removing the cause): Bilateral symmetric tender retroareolar glandular tissue in a man on anabolic steroids is typical of gynaecomastia, with a fan or flame-shaped density merging into fat. No biopsy is needed when the pattern is classic. Carcinoma is unilateral and eccentric. Pseudogynaecomastia contains no glandular density at all."}, {"id": 505, "domain": "breast", "question": "A 40-year-old woman has a screening mammogram showing a 7 mm oval, circumscribed mass in the upper outer quadrant of the right breast, near the axillary tail. A lucent notch is visible on one side, and it is seen on both views. Ultrasound shows a similar appearance. What is the most likely diagnosis and category?", "options": {"A": "Intramammary lymph node with a fatty hilum, benign finding, BI-RADS category 2", "B": "Papillary carcinoma, suspicious, BI-RADS category 4B with ultrasound-guided core biopsy", "C": "Fibroadenoma, probably benign, BI-RADS category 3 with follow-up mammography at six months", "D": "Epidermal inclusion cyst of skin origin, benign, BI-RADS category 2 with clinical skin examination"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Intramammary lymph node with a fatty hilum, benign finding, BI-RADS category 2): A small, oval, circumscribed mass with a fatty hilum notch in the upper outer quadrant near the axillary tail is a typical intramammary lymph node and is BI-RADS 2. Fibroadenoma lacks the notch, an inclusion cyst lies in the skin and is shown by tangential views, and papillary carcinoma would not look like a typical node."}, {"id": 513, "domain": "breast", "question": "A 56-year-old woman has architectural distortion on tomosynthesis with long thin spicules radiating from a small lucent centre. The appearance changes between projections, and no central mass is seen. Which diagnosis do these features favour over invasive carcinoma, and what is the main caution?", "options": {"A": "Tubular carcinoma, since low-grade tumours characteristically show a dense central mass", "B": "Sclerosing adenosis, which presents as a spiculated mass and needs no sampling", "C": "Post-surgical scar, where management is guided by the previous operative history", "D": "Radial scar, but sampling is required because carcinoma can coexist within the lesion"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Radial scar, but sampling is required because carcinoma can coexist within the lesion): A radiolucent centre, long thin spicules and variable appearance between views favour radial scar over carcinoma, which usually has a central dense mass. Radial scars may coexist with DCIS or invasive cancer, so tissue sampling remains necessary. A surgical scar needs a relevant history."}, {"id": 516, "domain": "breast", "question": "A 50-year-old woman has a newly diagnosed unifocal invasive ductal carcinoma. Preoperative MRI shows an additional 7 mm irregular enhancing mass in a different quadrant. Second-look targeted ultrasound and repeat tomosynthesis do not demonstrate any correlate. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "MRI-guided biopsy of the additional mass before the surgical plan is finalised", "B": "Order FDG PET-CT to decide whether the additional lesion is truly malignant", "C": "Repeat the MRI in six months to assess interval change in the second mass", "D": "Proceed with planned surgery and ignore the additional MRI finding as a false positive"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (MRI-guided biopsy of the additional mass before the surgical plan is finalised): An additional suspicious MRI lesion that could change surgery, such as converting breast conservation to mastectomy, needs histological confirmation. When second-look imaging finds no correlate, MRI-guided biopsy is the way to sample it. Ignoring, delaying or using PET-CT does not resolve it."}, {"id": 517, "domain": "breast", "question": "A 48-year-old woman at average risk has extremely dense breasts on mammography and no personal history of breast disease. She wishes to add supplemental screening to detect cancers hidden by breast density. Which modality detects the most additional cancers per round of screening in this setting?", "options": {"A": "Digital breast tomosynthesis, which largely removes the masking effect of dense tissue", "B": "Contrast-enhanced breast MRI, which finds the most mammographically occult cancers", "C": "Handheld ultrasound, which detects the most extra cancers with the fewest false positive recalls", "D": "Repeat two-view mammography at six months to increase sensitivity cumulatively"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Contrast-enhanced breast MRI, which finds the most mammographically occult cancers): In women with extremely dense breasts, supplemental MRI detects the highest number of additional cancers and reduces interval cancers, at the cost of more recalls than mammography alone. Ultrasound adds fewer cancers with more false positives, tomosynthesis helps but does not remove masking, and repeating mammography does not add sensitivity."}, {"id": 519, "domain": "breast", "question": "A 54-year-old woman with a 10 mm cluster of fine pleomorphic calcifications has stereotactic vacuum-assisted biopsy. Pathology shows columnar cell change without atypia. The specimen radiograph shows no calcifications in the cores, and the post-procedure mammogram shows the cluster is still present. What is the next step?", "options": {"A": "Repeat biopsy or surgical localisation excision, because the calcifications were not sampled", "B": "Arrange six month follow-up mammography because columnar cell change often accompanies calcification", "C": "Accept as concordant benign and resume routine screening in two years", "D": "Perform breast MRI to decide whether the cluster is worth resampling at all"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Repeat biopsy or surgical localisation excision, because the calcifications were not sampled): Fine pleomorphic calcifications are suspicious, and a result that does not contain the calcifications is a sampling failure, not benign concordance. The lesion remains unsampled and needs repeat biopsy or excision. Follow-up or MRI would leave a potentially malignant lesion undiagnosed."}, {"id": 522, "domain": "breast", "question": "A 50-year-old woman completed whole-breast radiotherapy after lumpectomy eight months ago. Mammography shows skin thickening of 3 mm, increased trabecular thickness, and mild overall increased density in the treated breast, with no mass and a stable scar. How should these findings be interpreted?", "options": {"A": "Expected radiotherapy changes that usually improve over time, with routine surveillance continuing", "B": "Early local recurrence, so mammographic follow-up at three months is advised", "C": "Infective mastitis, so breast ultrasound and a course of antibiotics follow", "D": "Fat necrosis at the scar, so ultrasound-guided core biopsy follows"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Expected radiotherapy changes that usually improve over time, with routine surveillance continuing): Skin thickening, coarsened trabeculae and mildly increased density are expected after whole-breast radiotherapy and usually settle over months to years. Early recurrence would show a new mass, pleomorphic calcification or progressive change, so short-interval follow-up is not needed. Mastitis would be painful with an acute course, and fat necrosis is a focal scar-related lesion rather than a diffuse change."}, {"id": 525, "domain": "breast", "question": "A 68-year-old woman has bilateral screening mammograms showing numerous coarse, smooth, rod-shaped calcifications more than 1 mm in width, oriented in lines radiating toward the nipple, with some lucent centres, and no associated mass. Which diagnosis fits, and what BI-RADS category applies?", "options": {"A": "Secretory calcifications of duct ectasia, benign, BI-RADS category 2", "B": "Coarse heterogeneous calcifications, intermediate suspicion, BI-RADS category 4B", "C": "Fine linear branching calcifications of DCIS, suspicious, BI-RADS category 4C", "D": "Vascular calcification along small arteries, benign, BI-RADS category 2 with no further imaging"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Secretory calcifications of duct ectasia, benign, BI-RADS category 2): Smooth, thick, rod-like calcifications that radiate toward the nipple are secretory calcifications of duct ectasia and are benign. Fine linear branching forms are thin, irregular and discontinuous, vascular calcification runs parallel tracks along vessels, and coarse heterogeneous calcifications are irregular and not rod-like."}, {"id": 527, "domain": "obstetrics_and_gynaecology", "question": "A 33-year-old woman has a positive urine test and mild lower abdominal discomfort. She is haemodynamically stable. Transvaginal ultrasound shows a thin endometrium with no intrauterine sac, no adnexal mass and no free fluid. Serum hCG is 900 IU/L. What is the most appropriate classification and management step?", "options": {"A": "Presumed ectopic pregnancy, proceeding directly to laparoscopy to confirm and remove the tube", "B": "Early intrauterine pregnancy too small to see, needing booking and a scan in two weeks", "C": "Complete miscarriage, with discharge and a urine test in three weeks", "D": "Pregnancy of unknown location, with repeat hCG at 48 hours and clinical review"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pregnancy of unknown location, with repeat hCG at 48 hours and clinical review): When no intrauterine or extra-uterine pregnancy is seen in a stable woman with a positive test, the correct label is pregnancy of unknown location. The hCG trend over 48 hours separates a growing intrauterine pregnancy, a failing pregnancy and an ectopic. Surgery without a visualised ectopic is premature, and a complete miscarriage requires documented hCG decline or a prior intrauterine sac."}, {"id": 529, "domain": "obstetrics_and_gynaecology", "question": "A 27-year-old woman presents after a positive pregnancy test with light bleeding. Transvaginal ultrasound shows a round intrauterine sac with a thickened echogenic rim and a mean sac diameter of 27 mm. There is no yolk sac and no embryo. Her uterus is anteverted and otherwise normal. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Possible early failure, not yet diagnostic, so a repeat scan in seven days", "B": "Incomplete miscarriage with retained products, rescanned once bleeding settles", "C": "Anembryonic pregnancy, diagnosed because the sac exceeds 25 mm without an embryo", "D": "Pseudosac of ectopic pregnancy, confirmed by adnexal assessment and serial hCG"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Anembryonic pregnancy, diagnosed because the sac exceeds 25 mm without an embryo): A mean sac diameter of 25 mm or more with no embryo is diagnostic of an anembryonic pregnancy, so waiting for a repeat scan is unnecessary. A pseudosac is small, central and lacks a true thickened decidual rim. Incomplete miscarriage shows irregular endometrial tissue rather than an intact round sac, and a repeat scan in a week applies only to sacs below the diagnostic threshold."}, {"id": 530, "domain": "obstetrics_and_gynaecology", "question": "A woman at nine weeks by dates has a first scan showing an intrauterine sac with a yolk sac but no embryo. The sac measures 14 mm. She is clinically stable. A repeat transvaginal scan is arranged. What is the earliest interval after which absence of an embryo with a heartbeat is diagnostic of failed pregnancy?", "options": {"A": "Seven days after the first scan, if the sac has grown by 1 mm daily", "B": "Eleven days after the first scan, when the sac with a yolk sac is unchanged", "C": "Fourteen days after the first scan, the rule for a sac lacking a yolk sac", "D": "Forty-eight hours after the first scan, if serum hCG fails to rise by more than half"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Eleven days after the first scan, when the sac with a yolk sac is unchanged): Consensus criteria give 14 days for a sac without a yolk sac and 11 days for a sac with a yolk sac when no embryo with a heartbeat develops. The 14 day interval is tempting but belongs to the first situation. Seven days or a 48 hour hCG check is not diagnostic and could cause a wrongly terminated viable pregnancy."}, {"id": 535, "domain": "obstetrics_and_gynaecology", "question": "At 19 weeks, a monochorionic diamniotic twin pregnancy shows twin A with a maximum vertical pocket of 1.0 cm and a small but visible bladder. Twin B has a maximum vertical pocket of 9.5 cm and a visible, distended bladder. Umbilical artery Doppler is normal in both. What is the most likely diagnosis and stage?", "options": {"A": "Twin anaemia polycythaemia sequence, diagnosed by discordant middle cerebral artery velocities", "B": "Twin reversed arterial perfusion sequence with an acardiac twin and absent heart", "C": "Twin-twin transfusion syndrome, Quintero stage I with a stuck donor twin", "D": "Selective growth restriction type I, with discordant weights but normal amniotic fluid in both"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Twin-twin transfusion syndrome, Quintero stage I with a stuck donor twin): Oligohydramnios with a maximum vertical pocket under 2 cm in one sac and polyhydramnios over 8 cm in the other, in a monochorionic pair, defines twin-twin transfusion syndrome. Stage I is present because the donor bladder is still visible and Doppler is normal. Selective growth restriction has no fluid discordance of this degree, and anaemia polycythaemia is defined by Doppler velocities."}, {"id": 536, "domain": "obstetrics_and_gynaecology", "question": "At 12 weeks, a twin pregnancy shows two separate fetal poles with a thick membrane between them. At the placental insertion, a triangular projection of placental tissue extends into the base of the membrane. There is a single apparent placental mass. What is the correct chorionicity and amnionicity?", "options": {"A": "Chorionicity indeterminate at this gestation, so reassess at 16 weeks before classifying", "B": "Dichorionic diamniotic, supported by the lambda sign at the membrane insertion", "C": "Monochorionic diamniotic, supported by a thin membrane with a T-shaped insertion", "D": "Monochorionic diamniotic, supported by a single apparent placental mass"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Dichorionic diamniotic, supported by the lambda sign at the membrane insertion): A triangular wedge of chorionic tissue at the membrane base is the lambda or twin-peak sign and indicates dichorionic diamniotic twins, even when the two placentas appear fused as one mass. The T sign has a thin membrane meeting the placenta at a right angle and indicates monochorionic twins. Chorionicity is reliably assigned at 11 to 14 weeks and should not be left until later."}, {"id": 537, "domain": "obstetrics_and_gynaecology", "question": "A 26-year-old woman at 14 weeks has a twin pregnancy. Ultrasound shows two fetuses within a single amniotic cavity with no dividing membrane. Colour Doppler demonstrates intermingled umbilical cords with a repeating pattern of arterial waveforms at two different heart rates. Which risk is most characteristic of this type of twin pregnancy?", "options": {"A": "Umbilical cord entanglement causing sudden intrauterine death of one or both fetuses", "B": "Conjoined twinning from late cleavage of the embryonic disc after day twelve", "C": "Twin-twin transfusion from a single shared chorion with fluid discordance between two sacs", "D": "Growth discordance caused by placental insufficiency in the fetus with a marginal cord insertion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Umbilical cord entanglement causing sudden intrauterine death of one or both fetuses): Monoamniotic twins share one sac, and the freely moving cords entangle, which is the dominant cause of unexpected fetal death. The sacs are not separate, so fluid discordance does not define the problem. Conjoined twins are fused, which is excluded by two separate bodies. Cord entanglement is recognised by intermingled cords and two different heart rates on Doppler."}, {"id": 543, "domain": "obstetrics_and_gynaecology", "question": "At 22 weeks, a fetus has a left-sided congenital diaphragmatic hernia. The stomach and bowel are in the chest, the heart is displaced to the right, and part of the liver is seen above the diaphragm. The lung-to-head ratio is measured. Which finding most strongly predicts a worse outcome?", "options": {"A": "Polyhydramnios with a mildly displaced heart and the stomach lying anteriorly in the chest", "B": "Stomach herniation with an observed to expected lung-to-head ratio above 45 percent", "C": "Bowel-only herniation with the stomach in a normal intra-abdominal position", "D": "Liver herniated into the chest together with a low observed to expected lung-to-head ratio"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Liver herniated into the chest together with a low observed to expected lung-to-head ratio): Intrathoracic liver together with a low observed to expected lung-to-head ratio indicates severe pulmonary hypoplasia and poor survival, and it guides consideration of fetal endoscopic tracheal occlusion. Stomach herniation with a good ratio is a weaker predictor. Polyhydramnios is common but does not measure lung volume, and bowel-only herniation is less severe."}, {"id": 546, "domain": "obstetrics_and_gynaecology", "question": "At 20 weeks, a scan shows a scalloped frontal bone, curved cerebellar hemispheres wrapping the brainstem with an obliterated cisterna magna, and a small head circumference. Mild ventriculomegaly is present, and the spine has not been fully assessed because of fetal position. What is the most likely underlying diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Dandy-Walker malformation with enlarged fossa and upturned vermis", "B": "Open spina bifida with Chiari II malformation features", "C": "Rhombencephalosynapsis with fused cerebellar hemispheres and no vermis", "D": "Aqueductal stenosis with isolated ventriculomegaly and normal fossa"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Open spina bifida with Chiari II malformation features): A scalloped frontal bone (lemon sign), curved cerebellar hemispheres (banana sign) and an obliterated cisterna magna form the cranial signature of open spinal dysraphism with Chiari II malformation. Dandy-Walker has an enlarged posterior fossa. Aqueductal stenosis has a normal posterior fossa, and rhombencephalosynapsis fuses the hemispheres without a small head and obliterated cisterna."}, {"id": 550, "domain": "obstetrics_and_gynaecology", "question": "A 17-year-old with primary amenorrhoea has normal secondary sexual characteristics and a 46,XX karyotype. Pelvic MRI shows normal ovaries, no identifiable uterus apart from tiny midline soft tissue remnants, and a short blind-ending vagina. Which additional finding is most commonly associated?", "options": {"A": "Gonadal malignancy risk from retained Y chromosome material", "B": "Polycystic ovarian morphology with hirsutism and raised circulating androgens", "C": "Left-sided cardiac outflow lesions, as in Turner syndrome", "D": "Renal anomalies, most often unilateral renal agenesis, with vertebral anomalies"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Renal anomalies, most often unilateral renal agenesis, with vertebral anomalies): Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser syndrome has normal ovaries and secondary sexual characteristics with an absent uterus and upper vagina. Renal anomalies, most often unilateral agenesis, and vertebral anomalies are the common associations. Turner features go with streak gonads, retained Y material needs a 46,XY karyotype, and polycystic ovarian morphology is not part of this syndrome."}, {"id": 552, "domain": "obstetrics_and_gynaecology", "question": "Hysterosalpingography in a woman with infertility shows a normal uterine cavity. The left tube fills and spills freely into the pelvis. The right tube is dilated, with a distal ampullary blockage and no peritoneal spill. Delayed films show contrast pooling within the right tube. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Tubal sterilisation with clips placed at the isthmic portion of the tube", "B": "Right hydrosalpinx caused by distal tubal occlusion at the ampulla", "C": "Right salpingitis isthmica nodosa with diverticula along the isthmus", "D": "Right tubal spasm at the cornual region, which resolves with antispasmodic medication"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Right hydrosalpinx caused by distal tubal occlusion at the ampulla): A tube dilated distally with pooling of contrast and no peritoneal spill is a hydrosalpinx from distal occlusion, which reduces the success of in vitro fertilisation and may need salpingectomy. Spasm affects the cornu and resolves. Isthmic nodosa shows small diverticula, and sterilisation clips cause a sharp proximal cut-off."}, {"id": 553, "domain": "obstetrics_and_gynaecology", "question": "A 27-year-old woman at 16 weeks has acute pelvic pain with low-grade fever. Non-contrast MRI shows a 7 cm intramural fibroid with peripheral high signal on T1 fat-suppressed images and a central area of low T2 signal. Which diagnosis fits best?", "options": {"A": "Uterine leiomyosarcoma, which shows irregular necrosis with markedly raised signal on diffusion imaging", "B": "Adenomyoma, which shows ill-defined margins and tiny cysts within the junctional zone", "C": "Red (carneous) degeneration of a fibroid, typical during pregnancy", "D": "Cystic degeneration of a fibroid, which appears uniformly bright on T2 with no T1 signal"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Red (carneous) degeneration of a fibroid, typical during pregnancy): Haemorrhagic infarction produces a T1 hyperintense rim from methaemoglobin with non-enhancing tissue, typical of red degeneration in pregnancy and sudden pain. Cystic degeneration is bright on T2 without T1 signal. Sarcoma is rare and has irregular enhancing solid tissue with restricted diffusion, while adenomyoma has ill-defined margins."}, {"id": 554, "domain": "obstetrics_and_gynaecology", "question": "A 42-year-old woman has heavy menstrual bleeding and a bulky uterus. Transvaginal ultrasound shows asymmetric thickening of the posterior myometrium, with small anechoic cysts and echogenic linear striations radiating from the endometrium. There are no discrete masses. Which feature best supports adenomyosis rather than leiomyoma?", "options": {"A": "Circumferential vascularity around a discrete mass on Doppler", "B": "Subendometrial linear striations with myometrial cysts and no distinct rounded margin", "C": "Hypoechoic nodules with rim shadowing in the anterior myometrium", "D": "Globular uterine enlargement with heterogeneous myometrial echotexture and normal endometrium"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Subendometrial linear striations with myometrial cysts and no distinct rounded margin): Subendometrial echogenic striations, myometrial cysts and ill-defined borders favour adenomyosis. Globular enlargement and heterogeneous echotexture occur in both adenomyosis and fibroids, so they do not discriminate. Rim shadowing from discrete nodules and circumferential vascularity around a mass are fibroid features."}, {"id": 556, "domain": "obstetrics_and_gynaecology", "question": "A 33-year-old woman with chronic pelvic pain and infertility has a transvaginal ultrasound. The left ovary contains a 5 cm unilocular cyst with homogeneous low-level internal echoes in a ground-glass pattern. There is no internal vascularity and no solid component. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Serous cystadenoma, which usually has thin septa and anechoic content", "B": "Endometrioma, a benign lesion consistent with O-RADS US category 2", "C": "Mature cystic teratoma, which contains echogenic fat and shadowing hair", "D": "Simple follicular cyst, which is anechoic with a thin imperceptible wall"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Endometrioma, a benign lesion consistent with O-RADS US category 2): A unilocular ovarian cyst with homogeneous ground-glass content and no vascular solid component is characteristic of an endometrioma. O-RADS US places a classic endometrioma under 10 cm in category 2, almost certainly benign. Teratomas show echogenic, shadowing material, and simple cysts are anechoic."}, {"id": 565, "domain": "obstetrics_and_gynaecology", "question": "A 52-year-old woman has a solid adnexal mass on ultrasound. Pelvic MRI shows a 5 cm homogeneously solid ovarian mass with very low signal on T2-weighted images and on high b-value diffusion-weighted images. There is mild enhancement, lower than the myometrium on dynamic contrast. Which O-RADS MRI score is appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Score 4, intermediate risk, which implies an enhancing solid component", "B": "Score 2, almost certainly benign, typical of fibrothecoma", "C": "Score 5, high risk, which implies a high-risk time-intensity curve", "D": "Score 3, low risk, which requires an intermediate time-intensity curve"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Score 2, almost certainly benign, typical of fibrothecoma): Solid tissue that is dark on T2 and dark on high b-value diffusion imaging, as in fibrothecoma, is O-RADS MRI score 2. Scores 3 to 5 depend on solid tissue enhancement relative to the myometrium and curve type. The mild enhancement below the myometrium supports a benign tissue."}, {"id": 575, "domain": "obstetrics_and_gynaecology", "question": "A 29-year-old woman with bleeding has a gestational sac in the cervical canal. To distinguish a cervical ectopic from a miscarriage in progress, the sonographer applies gentle pressure with the transvaginal probe. She is haemodynamically stable. Which finding favours a cervical ectopic pregnancy?", "options": {"A": "Sac fixed in place with peritrophoblastic flow, and a negative sliding sign", "B": "Sac in the lower cavity that deforms and descends with a Valsalva manoeuvre", "C": "Open internal os with a ragged, collapsed sac and echogenic debris", "D": "Sac sliding along the canal with gentle pressure and no surrounding vascularity"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Sac fixed in place with peritrophoblastic flow, and a negative sliding sign): A cervical ectopic is implanted in the canal wall, so the sac stays fixed under probe pressure and shows peritrophoblastic flow. A miscarriage in progress slides or deforms with pressure, lacks surrounding vascularity and often shows an open os with debris. A low intrauterine sac that moves with a Valsalva manoeuvre is also a miscarriage sign, not an ectopic one."}, {"id": 577, "domain": "obstetrics_and_gynaecology", "question": "A 35-year-old woman who conceived by embryo transfer at six weeks has lower abdominal pain. Ultrasound shows a live intrauterine pregnancy with a yolk sac and fetal pole. In the right adnexa, a separate extra-ovarian ring structure with a small amount of free fluid is also seen. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome, causing adnexal enlargement and free fluid after assisted conception", "B": "Hydrosalpinx, a tubular adnexal fluid structure that can coexist with pregnancy", "C": "Corpus luteum cyst, a common finding in early pregnancy after embryo transfer", "D": "Heterotopic pregnancy, with a viable intrauterine pregnancy and a coexisting tubal ectopic"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Heterotopic pregnancy, with a viable intrauterine pregnancy and a coexisting tubal ectopic): Assisted conception raises the chance of coexisting intrauterine and ectopic pregnancy, so a separate extra-ovarian ring with free fluid after a live intrauterine pregnancy is a tubal ectopic until proven otherwise. A corpus luteum lies within the ovary and has a vascular rim. Ovarian hyperstimulation enlarges both ovaries, and hydrosalpinx is tubular with incomplete folds. Prompt treatment protects the intrauterine pregnancy."}, {"id": 584, "domain": "paediatrics", "question": "A post-term infant with meconium-stained liquor is ventilated for hypoxia. Radiographs at birth show coarse patchy opacities with hyperinflation and flattened diaphragms. At 12 hours oxygen saturation falls abruptly with a new asymmetric hypertransradiant hemithorax. Which complication is the most likely cause of this sudden deterioration?", "options": {"A": "Air leak from air trapping behind partially obstructed small airways", "B": "Pulmonary haemorrhage with abrupt bilateral opacification and bloody tracheal secretions", "C": "Persistent pulmonary hypertension with right-to-left shunting across the ductus", "D": "Endotracheal tube migration into a main bronchus with contralateral lung collapse"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Air leak from air trapping behind partially obstructed small airways): Meconium causes ball-valve obstruction of small airways, so positive pressure ventilation readily produces pneumothorax, seen as an abrupt hypertransradiant hemithorax. Pulmonary hypertension and pulmonary haemorrhage can cause sudden desaturation but do not produce a lucent hemithorax. Tube migration causes collapse of the opposite lung rather than a lucent one."}, {"id": 585, "domain": "paediatrics", "question": "An infant born at 27 weeks remains oxygen dependent at 36 weeks corrected age after prolonged ventilation. The chest radiograph shows coarse reticular opacities, scattered cystic lucencies and hyperinflation. There are no linear lucencies radiating from the hila. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Pulmonary interstitial emphysema following recent barotrauma from ventilator pressures", "B": "Recurrent aspiration pneumonitis from gastro-oesophageal reflux with lower zone consolidation", "C": "Chronic lung disease of prematurity with fibrosis and emphysematous change", "D": "Persistent surfactant deficiency with homogeneous ground-glass opacification and low volumes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Chronic lung disease of prematurity with fibrosis and emphysematous change): Oxygen dependence at 36 weeks with a coarse reticular and bubbly pattern is the established form of bronchopulmonary dysplasia. Interstitial emphysema occurs in the first days and shows linear radiating lucencies. Surfactant deficiency presents in the first hours with low volumes. Aspiration produces dependent focal consolidation rather than diffuse coarse change."}, {"id": 598, "domain": "paediatrics", "question": "A 6-month-old girl has her first febrile urinary tract infection. A micturating cystourethrogram shows contrast filling the ureter and renal pelvis, with mild dilatation, a straight ureter and slightly rounded calyceal fornices. How is this reflux graded by the international classification?", "options": {"A": "Grade II, reaching the renal pelvis without dilatation of the collecting system", "B": "Grade III, with mild to moderate dilatation of the collecting system", "C": "Grade V, with gross dilatation and loss of the papillary impressions", "D": "Grade IV, with ureteric tortuosity and obliterated sharp angles of the fornices"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Grade III, with mild to moderate dilatation of the collecting system): Grade III reflux fills the ureter and pelvis with mild to moderate dilatation and minimal blunting of fornices while the ureter stays straight. Grade II lacks dilatation. Grade IV adds moderate dilatation with tortuosity and loss of sharp fornix angles but preserved papillary impressions. Grade V shows gross dilatation and loss of papillae."}, {"id": 605, "domain": "paediatrics", "question": "A 7-year-old boy has a painless limp. Radiographs show a small, dense, fragmented left femoral epiphysis. The lateral pillar is assessed on the AP view at the stage of maximal fragmentation. There is no history of trauma, and blood tests including inflammatory markers are normal. He is otherwise healthy and active. Which finding indicates the worst prognosis?", "options": {"A": "Mild epiphyseal flattening with preserved lateral pillar height", "B": "Isolated crescent sign beneath the anterolateral subchondral bone", "C": "Lateral pillar height loss greater than half of the original height", "D": "Age of five years at presentation with unilateral involvement"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lateral pillar height loss greater than half of the original height): The Herring lateral pillar classification predicts outcome: collapse of more than half of the lateral pillar height (group C) has the worst prognosis. A crescent sign marks the subchondral fracture in early disease. Preserved pillar height (group A) and younger age at onset carry better outcomes."}, {"id": 606, "domain": "paediatrics", "question": "A 6-week-old girl born breech has a hip ultrasound. The alpha angle is 45 degrees, the beta angle is 85 degrees, and the femoral head is displaced upward from the acetabulum but still contained by a cartilaginous roof. How is this hip classified by the Graf method?", "options": {"A": "Graf type IIa, physiological immaturity expected to resolve without treatment", "B": "Graf type IIc, a critical hip with a narrow beta angle", "C": "Graf type III, a dislocated hip with the alpha angle below 43 degrees", "D": "Graf type D, a decentring hip requiring early treatment"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Graf type D, a decentring hip requiring early treatment): An alpha angle of 43 to 49 degrees places the hip in the dysplastic range, and a beta angle above 77 degrees with early displacement identifies type D, a decentring hip. Type IIc has the same alpha range but a beta angle below 77 degrees. Type IIa is a physiological variant at a younger age. Type III has a dislocated head with alpha below 43 degrees."}, {"id": 607, "domain": "paediatrics", "question": "A girl is born at term in breech presentation, and the clinical hip examination is normal. The parents ask when ultrasound screening of her hips should be performed to avoid misleading results from transient ligamentous laxity. When is the optimal time to scan?", "options": {"A": "At two weeks of age, after the first paediatric review", "B": "At about six weeks of age, when physiological laxity has resolved", "C": "At three months, once the femoral epiphysis has started to ossify", "D": "Before discharge from the ward, since breech presentation needs immediate imaging"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (At about six weeks of age, when physiological laxity has resolved): Early scans overcall dysplasia because maternal hormones loosen the ligaments, so screening for a breech girl with a normal examination is best at about six weeks. By about four months the femoral head begins to ossify and ultrasound becomes less reliable. Scanning at birth or two weeks risks false positives."}, {"id": 611, "domain": "paediatrics", "question": "A 3-month-old with suspected abuse has an initial skeletal survey that shows no definite fracture but equivocal rib irregularity. Child protection is involved. The infant was admitted for observation, appears otherwise well, and has a normal examination. Medical imaging has excluded intracranial injury. When should limited follow-up radiography be performed to increase sensitivity?", "options": {"A": "Repeat at 48 hours because new bone forms within a day", "B": "Repeat at eight weeks once remodelling is complete", "C": "Repeat limited survey at 11 to 14 days to demonstrate healing fractures", "D": "Technetium bone scan the next day in place of repeat radiographs"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Repeat limited survey at 11 to 14 days to demonstrate healing fractures): Healing callus makes subtle fractures visible after about two weeks, so a limited repeat survey at 11 to 14 days improves detection. At 48 hours callus has not formed, and by eight weeks many injuries have remodelled. Bone scintigraphy is not a substitute for radiographic follow-up."}, {"id": 614, "domain": "paediatrics", "question": "A 27-week infant has cranial ultrasound on day three. Echogenic blood fills and distends the left lateral ventricle with a cast of clot, and the adjacent white matter has normal echogenicity. He received surfactant at birth and is on nasal continuous positive airway pressure. What grade of germinal matrix haemorrhage is this?", "options": {"A": "Grade II, intraventricular blood without enlargement of the ventricle", "B": "Grade IV, periventricular haemorrhagic infarction of the adjacent white matter", "C": "Grade I, subependymal haemorrhage confined to the germinal matrix", "D": "Grade III, intraventricular haemorrhage with ventricular dilatation present"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Grade III, intraventricular haemorrhage with ventricular dilatation present): Blood within a distended ventricle without parenchymal involvement is grade III on the Papile scheme. Grade I is limited to the germinal matrix. Grade II has intraventricular blood without enlargement. Grade IV requires echogenic haemorrhagic infarction in the adjacent white matter."}, {"id": 615, "domain": "paediatrics", "question": "A newborn has a lumbosacral open neural tube defect repaired on day one. Cranial ultrasound and MRI are performed for hydrocephalus. There is a lumbosacral swelling covered by a thin membrane with leaking fluid, and the legs show reduced movement. Head circumference is rising above the expected centile. Which posterior fossa appearance is characteristically associated with this spinal defect?", "options": {"A": "Absent vermis with a molar tooth sign and elongated superior cerebellar peduncles", "B": "Small posterior fossa with tonsillar and vermian herniation through the foramen magnum", "C": "Isolated tonsillar descent below the foramen magnum with an otherwise normal vermis and fourth ventricle", "D": "Cystic fourth ventricle with an elevated torcula and hypoplastic vermis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Small posterior fossa with tonsillar and vermian herniation through the foramen magnum): Open myelomeningocele leaks cerebrospinal fluid in utero, leaving a small posterior fossa with caudal herniation of the tonsils and vermis through the foramen magnum, which is Chiari II. Chiari I has isolated tonsillar descent without vermian herniation. Dandy-Walker has an enlarged fossa, and the molar tooth sign marks Joubert syndrome."}, {"id": 620, "domain": "paediatrics", "question": "An 18-month-old has leukocoria. Ultrasound shows a calcified intraocular mass, and bilateral disease is suspected with a possible germline RB1 mutation. The family has no known history of eye disease, and the child is otherwise well. Staging of the orbits and brain is planned. Which imaging study should be avoided if possible in this child?", "options": {"A": "MRI of the orbits under anaesthesia, owing to neurotoxicity concerns", "B": "MRI of the pineal region under sedation, owing to airway risk", "C": "Contrast MRI of the brain, owing to gadolinium retention", "D": "CT of the orbits, owing to raised risk of radiation-induced second cancers"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (CT of the orbits, owing to raised risk of radiation-induced second cancers): Children with germline RB1 mutations are highly susceptible to radiation-induced second malignancies, so CT of the orbits is avoided where MRI can answer the question. Anaesthetic and sedation risks are real but are accepted because MRI gives radiation-free staging of the optic nerve and brain. Gadolinium is used when needed for nerve invasion and trilateral disease, so it does not justify skipping MRI."}, {"id": 623, "domain": "paediatrics", "question": "A radiographer asks whether lead shielding should be placed over the gonads during a routine paediatric pelvic radiograph for a limp. The child is 7 years old with no history of trauma, and the radiographer has a lead shield ready. The department protocol predates the most recent national radiation protection statements. What is the present recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Recommended for boys only, using a shaped contact shield over the testes", "B": "Recommended routinely for children under ten, placed over the ovaries within the field", "C": "Recommended only when the child cannot be accurately collimated or immobilised", "D": "Not routinely recommended, as shields may obscure anatomy and add little benefit"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Not routinely recommended, as shields may obscure anatomy and add little benefit): Modern guidance holds that gonad shields placed in the field can hide pathology and trigger repeat exposures, and internal scatter limits their protective effect while gonad dose from modern equipment is low. Sex-based or age-based shielding policies belong to older practice. Poor collimation is corrected by technique rather than by a shield, so routine shielding is no longer advised."}, {"id": 627, "domain": "paediatrics", "question": "A 14-year-old has persistent knee pain and swelling. Radiographs show an aggressive lesion in the distal femur with cortical destruction and a soft tissue mass. Osteosarcoma and Ewing sarcoma are considered. Alkaline phosphatase and lactate dehydrogenase are raised, and there is no fever or erythema. MRI shows a large soft tissue component with marrow replacement. Which feature favours osteosarcoma?", "options": {"A": "Large soft tissue mass with minimal bone destruction in a flat bone", "B": "Diaphyseal permeative lesion with lamellated onion-skin periosteal reaction", "C": "Eccentric lucent lesion with a sclerotic rim and no periosteal response", "D": "Metaphyseal location with ossified tumour matrix and sunburst periosteal reaction"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Metaphyseal location with ossified tumour matrix and sunburst periosteal reaction): Osteosarcoma typically arises in the metaphysis around the knee and produces osteoid, seen as dense matrix and spiculated sunburst reaction with Codman triangles. Ewing sarcoma is more diaphyseal with onion-skin periosteal reaction. A sclerotic-rimmed lesion without periosteal response is benign."}, {"id": 631, "domain": "paediatrics", "question": "A 3-week-old infant has progressive tachypnoea. The chest radiograph shows a hyperlucent, expanded left upper zone with attenuated vessels, compression of the left lower lobe and mediastinal shift to the right. He was initially well, but saturations have fallen gradually. There was no ventilation or meconium, and heart sounds are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Congenital lobar overinflation of the left upper lobe", "B": "Tension pneumothorax with complete collapse of the adjacent lung", "C": "Congenital pulmonary airway malformation type 2 with multiple small cysts", "D": "Pulmonary sequestration with systemic arterial supply from the thoracic aorta"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Congenital lobar overinflation of the left upper lobe): Progressive overinflation of one lobe, most often the left upper lobe, with stretched vessels and mass effect, is characteristic. Sequestration has an aortic feeder, and an airway malformation is cystic. A tension pneumothorax lacks vessels and shows lung collapse toward the hilum."}, {"id": 633, "domain": "paediatrics", "question": "An 8-year-old has right iliac fossa pain, fever and rebound tenderness. Graded compression ultrasound is used to examine the right lower quadrant. The child is febrile at 38.2 degrees and lies still with the right hip flexed. Which finding is the most specific for acute appendicitis?", "options": {"A": "Thickened terminal ileum with preserved wall stratification and a normal appendix", "B": "Mesenteric lymph nodes of 8 mm short axis in the right iliac fossa", "C": "Non-compressible blind-ending tubular structure with outer diameter above 6 mm", "D": "Visualised appendix measuring 5 mm that compresses fully under the probe"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Non-compressible blind-ending tubular structure with outer diameter above 6 mm): A fixed, non-compressible blind-ending tube wider than about 6 mm, often with hyperaemic wall and surrounding inflamed fat, is diagnostic. A compressible appendix of normal calibre excludes appendicitis, and adenitis or terminal ileal thickening point to alternative causes of pain."}, {"id": 635, "domain": "paediatrics", "question": "A 5-week-old has conjugated jaundice and pale stools. The stools are acholic and the liver is firm. Ultrasound after a four hour fast is performed to look for features of biliary atresia. Birth weight was normal, bilirubin is predominantly conjugated, and gamma-glutamyl transferase is elevated. Which finding is most specific?", "options": {"A": "Dilated common bile duct of 10 mm with intrahepatic ductal dilatation", "B": "Echogenic periportal fibrous tissue anterior to the portal vein bifurcation", "C": "Hepatomegaly with coarse echotexture and a hypoechoic periportal halo", "D": "Gallbladder contracting normally after a feed, suggesting patent bile ducts"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Echogenic periportal fibrous tissue anterior to the portal vein bifurcation): The triangular cord sign, an echogenic fibrous remnant of the extrahepatic duct anterior to the portal vein, is highly specific. A dilated duct suggests a choledochal cyst, normal contractility argues against atresia, and coarse hepatomegaly with periportal halo is nonspecific and fits hepatitis."}, {"id": 636, "domain": "paediatrics", "question": "A 28-week infant has a routine cranial ultrasound at four weeks of age. It shows multiple small cysts in the periventricular white matter adjacent to the lateral ventricular trigones. She was born after prolonged rupture of membranes with chorioamnionitis and had an episode of hypotension. What is the diagnosis and its usual neurological consequence?", "options": {"A": "Cystic periventricular leukomalacia, associated with later spastic diplegia", "B": "Grade IV haemorrhage with periventricular haemorrhagic infarction seen on ultrasound", "C": "Subependymal pseudocysts at the caudothalamic groove, a benign normal variant", "D": "Congenital cytomegalovirus infection with lenticulostriate vasculopathy and calcification"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cystic periventricular leukomalacia, associated with later spastic diplegia): Multiple cysts in periventricular white matter, developing a few weeks after an hypoperfusion injury, indicate cystic leukomalacia, which damages corticospinal fibres to the legs and leads to spastic diplegia. Haemorrhagic infarction is echogenic initially, and subependymal pseudocysts lie at the caudothalamic groove."}, {"id": 638, "domain": "interventional_and_multisystem", "question": "A 74-year-old man has surveillance CT six months after endovascular aneurysm repair. The arterial phase shows no contrast outside the graft, but the delayed phase shows contrast within the sac adjacent to a patent inferior mesenteric artery and a lumbar vessel. The sac diameter is unchanged. Which endoleak type is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Type II endoleak from retrograde flow through lumbar and mesenteric branches", "B": "Type III endoleak from a graft fabric tear or modular junction separation", "C": "Type I endoleak from an inadequate seal at the proximal or distal landing zone", "D": "Type IV endoleak from graft porosity, usually seen on the completion angiogram"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Type II endoleak from retrograde flow through lumbar and mesenteric branches): Contrast that appears only on delayed imaging near patent branch vessels, with a stable sac, is the typical type II pattern from retrograde branch filling. A type I leak would show arterial phase contrast at an attachment site and needs prompt treatment. A type III leak arises at a fabric defect or junction and also fills early. Type IV porosity leak is a brief finding at deployment and resolves."}, {"id": 641, "domain": "interventional_and_multisystem", "question": "A 58-year-old man with alcohol related cirrhosis and refractory variceal bleeding undergoes TIPS. Two weeks later he develops confusion and a flapping tremor. Liver function is unchanged and Doppler shows a patent shunt with high velocities. Which is the most likely explanation and next step?", "options": {"A": "Shunt stenosis with recurrent portal hypertension, needing venography and stent revision", "B": "Hypoxic liver injury after the procedure, needing supportive care and observation", "C": "Wernicke encephalopathy from alcohol related thiamine deficiency, treated with intravenous thiamine", "D": "Hepatic encephalopathy from diversion of portal blood, treated medically first"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hepatic encephalopathy from diversion of portal blood, treated medically first): New encephalopathy after TIPS reflects diversion of portal blood past the liver and is the commonest significant complication. High velocities in a patent shunt do not indicate stenosis, and stenosis would present with bleeding or ascites rather than a flapping tremor. Wernicke encephalopathy causes ophthalmoplegia and ataxia, and unchanged liver function argues against hypoxic injury. Treatment is lactulose and rifaximin, with shunt reduction if refractory."}, {"id": 646, "domain": "interventional_and_multisystem", "question": "A 62-year-old man has a 3 cm hepatocellular carcinoma abutting the right portal vein branch. Radiofrequency ablation achieves incomplete treatment on follow up CT, with residual enhancement along the margin facing the vessel. Which phenomenon best explains the residual tumour?", "options": {"A": "Skin burn at the grounding pad site reducing delivered current", "B": "Tissue carbonisation from excessive power, producing a hypodense charred margin", "C": "Heat sink effect from adjacent flowing blood cooling the ablation zone", "D": "Tumour desiccation reducing conductivity and halting energy delivery early"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Heat sink effect from adjacent flowing blood cooling the ablation zone): Flowing blood in a large adjacent vessel carries heat away, leaving residual tumour at that margin, which is the heat sink effect. Desiccation and carbonisation limit energy spread in general but do not account for failure localised to the vessel. Pad burns are a skin complication, not a cause of incomplete ablation."}, {"id": 650, "domain": "interventional_and_multisystem", "question": "A 28-year-old woman on the oral contraceptive pill presents with acute swelling of the entire left leg. Venous duplex confirms extensive iliofemoral thrombosis. Contrast CT venography shows the left common iliac vein compressed between the right common iliac artery and the lumbar spine. Which diagnosis fits?", "options": {"A": "May-Thurner syndrome with iliac vein compression predisposing to left sided thrombosis", "B": "Popliteal vein entrapment from the medial head of gastrocnemius", "C": "Nutcracker syndrome with left renal vein entrapment between the aorta and superior mesenteric artery", "D": "Pelvic congestion syndrome from incompetent ovarian veins causing chronic pelvic pain"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (May-Thurner syndrome with iliac vein compression predisposing to left sided thrombosis): Compression of the left common iliac vein by the overlying right common iliac artery is May-Thurner syndrome, which favours left iliofemoral thrombosis, especially with oestrogen exposure. Nutcracker involves the left renal vein. Popliteal entrapment concerns the artery or vein behind the knee. Pelvic congestion presents with pain and ovarian vein reflux."}, {"id": 652, "domain": "interventional_and_multisystem", "question": "A 76-year-old woman with an eGFR of 25 mL/min/1.73 m2 and type 2 diabetes needs contrast enhanced CT for suspected mesenteric ischaemia. She is euvolaemic, passes urine normally and takes no nephrotoxic drugs apart from her usual medicines. Which strategy best reduces the risk of contrast associated acute kidney injury?", "options": {"A": "Give oral N-acetylcysteine alone as the primary preventive measure before scanning", "B": "Proceed because the study is urgent, with isotonic saline volume expansion", "C": "Withhold the scan until eGFR recovers above 45 regardless of the clinical picture", "D": "Use furosemide diuresis to flush the contrast load from the kidneys"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Proceed because the study is urgent, with isotonic saline volume expansion): For an urgent diagnosis the benefit outweighs the risk, and prophylaxis with isotonic saline is the evidence based measure in patients with eGFR below 30. N-acetylcysteine lacks reliable benefit. Furosemide induced diuresis can cause volume depletion and harm. Delay in suspected mesenteric ischaemia would be dangerous."}, {"id": 653, "domain": "interventional_and_multisystem", "question": "A 60-year-old man on haemodialysis with an eGFR of 8 requires contrast enhanced MR angiography of the legs. The department stocks both linear and macrocyclic gadolinium agents. He has no residual urine output and attends dialysis three times weekly, and the referrer insists on contrast. Which choice best minimises the risk of nephrogenic systemic fibrosis?", "options": {"A": "A group I linear agent at half dose, since dose reduction removes the risk", "B": "Any agent at standard dose, because dialysis patients are protected by clearance", "C": "A linear agent followed by emergency dialysis within one hour of injection", "D": "A macrocyclic group II agent at the lowest diagnostic dose"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A macrocyclic group II agent at the lowest diagnostic dose): Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis is linked mostly to group I linear agents in severe renal impairment. Group II macrocyclic agents carry very low risk and are used at the lowest effective dose. Reducing the dose of a group I agent does not remove risk. Dialysis after injection is not a reliable protection, and standard use of any agent is inappropriate."}, {"id": 656, "domain": "interventional_and_multisystem", "question": "A 64-year-old man had a lengthy cardiac ablation with extensive fluoroscopy. Three weeks later he presents with a well demarcated area of erythema and dry desquamation over the right posterior chest, which later ulcerates. Which radiation effect is this, and what feature supports it?", "options": {"A": "Stochastic skin effect with no threshold, appearing at random skin sites", "B": "Contact dermatitis from the electrode pads, spread over the whole back", "C": "Deterministic skin injury with a dose threshold, localised to the beam entry site", "D": "Radiation recall reaction triggered by a drug given after a prior radiotherapy course"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Deterministic skin injury with a dose threshold, localised to the beam entry site): A localised injury at the entry site, appearing weeks after prolonged fluoroscopy, is a deterministic effect with a dose threshold, with transient erythema from about 2 Gy. Stochastic effects have no threshold and do not cause tissue reactions. Recall needs prior radiotherapy. Pad dermatitis would follow the pad outline."}, {"id": 657, "domain": "interventional_and_multisystem", "question": "A 12-year-old boy has multiple cafe au lait macules, axillary freckling and a soft tissue mass along the left thigh described on MRI as bright on T2 with a central low signal dot pattern. A visual screen shows an enlarged optic nerve. Which diagnosis fits best?", "options": {"A": "McCune-Albright syndrome with polyostotic fibrous dysplasia and endocrine abnormalities", "B": "Neurofibromatosis type 2 with bilateral vestibular schwannomas and meningiomas", "C": "Tuberous sclerosis with subependymal nodules and renal angiomyolipomas", "D": "Neurofibromatosis type 1 with plexiform neurofibroma and probable optic pathway glioma"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Neurofibromatosis type 1 with plexiform neurofibroma and probable optic pathway glioma): Cafe au lait macules, axillary freckling, a plexiform neurofibroma with the target sign and an optic pathway glioma are features of neurofibromatosis type 1. Type 2 features bilateral vestibular schwannomas and meningiomas. Tuberous sclerosis has hypomelanotic macules and cortical tubers. McCune-Albright has irregular cafe au lait macules and fibrous dysplasia."}, {"id": 663, "domain": "interventional_and_multisystem", "question": "A 34-year-old woman with erythema nodosum and arthralgia has a chest radiograph showing bilateral symmetrical hilar enlargement with right paratracheal widening. She is otherwise well. CT shows perilymphatic micronodules in the upper zones. Which diagnosis and radiographic stage are most likely?", "options": {"A": "Lymphoma, with asymmetric hilar nodes and central necrosis in the nodes", "B": "Tuberculosis, with unilateral hilar nodes and associated consolidation", "C": "Silicosis, with eggshell nodal calcification and lower zone nodules", "D": "Sarcoidosis, Scadding stage 1, with isolated nodal enlargement"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Sarcoidosis, Scadding stage 1, with isolated nodal enlargement): Symmetric bilateral hilar and right paratracheal nodes with erythema nodosum make Lofgren syndrome and sarcoidosis likely, and node enlargement alone on the radiograph is stage 1. Perilymphatic nodules favour the upper zones. Lymphoma tends to be asymmetric, tuberculosis typically unilateral, and silicosis has upper zone nodules with eggshell calcification."}, {"id": 669, "domain": "interventional_and_multisystem", "question": "A 47-year-old woman has sinusitis, haemoptysis and a raised creatinine. CT shows multiple bilateral lung nodules, several cavitating with thick walls, and subglottic narrowing. ANCA with cytoplasmic pattern is positive. Urinalysis shows blood and protein, and chest radiography earlier showed bilateral opacities. Which diagnosis best explains the findings?", "options": {"A": "Granulomatosis with polyangiitis with upper airway and pulmonary involvement", "B": "Septic emboli from endocarditis with peripheral wedge shaped cavities", "C": "Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis with asthma and eosinophilia", "D": "Goodpasture disease with diffuse alveolar haemorrhage and linear glomerular staining"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Granulomatosis with polyangiitis with upper airway and pulmonary involvement): Sinus disease, cavitating lung nodules, subglottic stenosis and renal impairment with cytoplasmic ANCA indicate granulomatosis with polyangiitis. Eosinophilic granulomatosis requires asthma and eosinophilia. Goodpasture disease causes alveolar haemorrhage without cavitating nodules. Septic emboli need an infective source."}, {"id": 671, "domain": "interventional_and_multisystem", "question": "A 22-year-old thin woman has FDG PET-CT in winter for lymphoma staging. There is symmetrical uptake in the supraclavicular, paravertebral and perirenal regions. Low dose CT shows these areas to be fat attenuation without nodal enlargement. What is the most likely explanation?", "options": {"A": "Inflammatory uptake from recent infection of the upper respiratory tract", "B": "Lymphomatous involvement of the supraclavicular and paravertebral lymph node chains", "C": "Muscular uptake from tension, reduced by relaxing the neck muscles", "D": "Physiological brown adipose tissue uptake, reduced by warming before injection"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Physiological brown adipose tissue uptake, reduced by warming before injection): Symmetrical FDG uptake in fat attenuation tissue in cold conditions in a young thin patient is brown adipose tissue. Warming the patient and pre-scan measures reduce it. Lymphoma would show soft tissue nodes. Muscle uptake corresponds to muscle attenuation. Infection gives nodal enlargement."}, {"id": 673, "domain": "interventional_and_multisystem", "question": "A 60-year-old man has completed chemoradiotherapy for oesophageal cancer. His clinician requests an FDG PET-CT two weeks after treatment to assess response. The referring oncologist wants to know whether surgery is needed, and the patient is clinically improving with swallowing now easier. Which advice is most appropriate regarding timing and interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Delay imaging until 12 months so that inflammatory change has fully resolved", "B": "Proceed now because early uptake reliably reflects residual viable tumour", "C": "Proceed now but interpret uptake above liver as complete metabolic response", "D": "Delay until about 12 weeks to limit false positive inflammatory uptake"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Delay until about 12 weeks to limit false positive inflammatory uptake): Radiation and chemotherapy cause inflammatory uptake that mimics tumour for weeks, so response PET is typically delayed about 12 weeks after chemoradiotherapy. Waiting 12 months is unnecessary and delays management. Uptake above liver is not a complete response."}, {"id": 674, "domain": "interventional_and_multisystem", "question": "A 35-year-old driver has a high speed frontal crash and an initial pan-scan. Arterial phase CT shows a contour irregularity and an intimal flap at the aortic isthmus just distal to the left subclavian artery, with a small mediastinal haematoma. Which is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Ductus diverticulum, a smooth bulge at the anteromedial aortic isthmus", "B": "Penetrating atherosclerotic ulcer with focal contrast outpouching in a calcified arch", "C": "Blunt traumatic aortic injury, graded by the Society for Vascular Surgery", "D": "Aortic dissection type A, from a spontaneous intimal tear in the ascending aorta"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Blunt traumatic aortic injury, graded by the Society for Vascular Surgery): A flap or irregular contour at the isthmus with mediastinal haematoma after deceleration is blunt traumatic aortic injury. Spontaneous type A dissection begins in the ascending aorta. Penetrating ulcers occur in atherosclerotic disease. A ductus diverticulum is smooth without a flap or haematoma."}, {"id": 678, "domain": "interventional_and_multisystem", "question": "A 38-year-old woman with cystic fibrosis has recurrent massive haemoptysis of 300 mL. CT angiography shows hypertrophied tortuous bronchial arteries supplying the right upper lobe bronchiectasis. Bronchoscopy localised bleeding to that lobe. She is haemodynamically stable on oxygen, with no coagulopathy, and the thoracic surgeons agree that intervention should be attempted. What is the best treatment?", "options": {"A": "Bronchoscopic balloon tamponade of the right upper lobe bronchus as definitive therapy", "B": "Bronchial artery embolisation, avoiding the anterior spinal artery origin", "C": "Nebulised tranexamic acid with repeat bronchoscopy as definitive treatment", "D": "Surgical ligation of the hypertrophied bronchial arteries through thoracotomy as first-line therapy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Bronchial artery embolisation, avoiding the anterior spinal artery origin): Massive haemoptysis in bronchiectasis usually arises from hypertrophied bronchial arteries at systemic pressure, and embolisation is first line. Spinal artery origin from the intercostobronchial trunk must be identified to avoid cord ischaemia. Surgery is a later option, balloon tamponade is a temporising measure, and nebulised tranexamic acid helps mild bleeding only."}, {"id": 682, "domain": "interventional_and_multisystem", "question": "A 66-year-old man develops a pulsatile groin mass two days after femoral access for coronary angiography. Duplex ultrasound shows a 2 cm pseudoaneurysm with a 4 mm neck and a to and fro waveform in the neck. He is stable. Which treatment is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Observation without intervention for a pseudoaneurysm of this size and neck width", "B": "Open surgical repair as the sole effective option for post catheter sacs", "C": "Ultrasound guided compression for 2 minutes, then discharge the patient", "D": "Ultrasound guided thrombin injection into the sac with monitoring of distal flow"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ultrasound guided thrombin injection into the sac with monitoring of distal flow): A pseudoaneurysm with a narrow neck is well suited to thrombin injection under ultrasound guidance, which achieves thrombosis in most cases. Compression takes much longer than a couple of minutes and is uncomfortable. Surgery is reserved for failures or wide necks. Pseudoaneurysms over about 2 cm with symptoms are not safe to observe."}, {"id": 688, "domain": "interventional_and_multisystem", "question": "A 62-year-old smoker has facial swelling, dilated chest wall veins and stridor. CT shows a right paratracheal mass encasing and occluding the SVC with collateral veins. There is no tissue diagnosis. Her oxygen saturation is 94 percent on air, and the oncology team is awaiting a decision before any treatment. Which management is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Biopsy of the mass first and defer treatment until histology is reported", "B": "Urgent SVC stent placement for airway compromise, then tissue diagnosis and treatment", "C": "Systemic anticoagulation alone, then review in one week with repeat cross sectional imaging", "D": "Empirical radiotherapy before any tissue is obtained, to avoid delay in treatment"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Urgent SVC stent placement for airway compromise, then tissue diagnosis and treatment): SVC obstruction with stridor is an emergency, and stenting gives rapid symptom relief, with tissue diagnosis obtained afterwards. Empirical radiotherapy before biopsy can compromise histology. Anticoagulation alone does not relieve a malignant obstruction. Waiting for histology delays relief of the airway."}, {"id": 691, "domain": "interventional_and_multisystem", "question": "A 66-year-old man is found to have an infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm measuring 4.2 cm on ultrasound screening. He is asymptomatic and fit, a former smoker with hypertension, and his blood pressure is controlled with medication. According to common surveillance guidance, what is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "CT angiography for stent graft sizing and a repair date in three months", "B": "Repeat ultrasound at 5 years because this size has a negligible growth rate", "C": "Repeat ultrasound at about 12 months with cardiovascular risk management", "D": "Elective endovascular repair now, because the diameter exceeds the 4 cm threshold"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Repeat ultrasound at about 12 months with cardiovascular risk management): Aneurysms below 5.5 cm in men are under surveillance, and the interval for 3.0 to 4.4 cm is generally annual (some guidance uses 12 months for 3.0 to 4.4). Repair is considered at 5.5 cm, or with rapid growth or symptoms. A 5 year interval is too long at this size, and planning for repair is premature."}, {"id": 695, "domain": "interventional_and_multisystem", "question": "A 55-year-old woman with Hodgkin lymphoma has a PET-CT after chemotherapy including growth factor support. There is diffuse intense uptake throughout the marrow, spleen with no focal bone lesions, and no change from nodal disease. Which explanation is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Diffuse marrow infiltration indicating true stage IV progression of lymphoma", "B": "Sickle cell disease with extramedullary haematopoiesis in the spleen and marrow", "C": "Reactive marrow and splenic uptake from colony stimulating factor therapy", "D": "Hepatic steatosis causing a global increase in marrow metabolism"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reactive marrow and splenic uptake from colony stimulating factor therapy): Growth factor therapy stimulates marrow and spleen, causing diffuse, symmetrical uptake that can mimic marrow involvement. This is reactive and usually resolves. Focal lesions or unexplained uptake in a patient not given growth factor would raise concern. Steatosis and sickle cell disease do not fit."}];
